{
  "title": "Capacity Planning Brief: Agent Host Sizing",
  "executive_summary": "Field-note evidence indicates that an orchestration host's cost per workflow is driven by the tool work its agents perform, not by the model layer. Across the topic435 operations corpus, regressions were consistently traced to contention on specific subsystem pools at concrete concurrency thresholds — e.g., pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas [chunk-26128], quantization contention at 2,522 concurrent medians [chunk-26110], and attention contention at 3,964 concurrent tokens [chunk-26122] — and each was resolved by sharding the offending pool (78-way, 18-way, and 22-way respectively). Capacity evaluations confirm headroom exists when utilization is held low: one configuration sustained 1,149 backlog/sec at under 68% capacity utilization, while another sustained 980 shards/sec at under 28% shard utilization [chunk-26136]. The clear recommendation is to plan agent-host capacity around measured tool-work throughput and per-pool concurrency limits, provisioning pools for sharding headroom rather than scaling model-tier resources.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Background: Cost Follows Tool Work, Not the Model",
      "content": "The governing premise of this brief, taken directly from the field notes, is that an orchestration host's cost per workflow is set by the tool work its agents do, not by the model. The retrieved operational records support this framing: none of the recorded fixes touch model weights or inference quality; every regression is attributed to a contention point in the surrounding execution machinery — pipelines, sampling pools, attention pools, quantization pools, socket and queue layers, schedulers, and replica buffers. For example, latency, queue, logit, temperature, tensor, numa, prefill, socket, affinity, bandwidth, saturation, checkpoint, and gradient regressions are all documented as contention events resolved by pool sharding [chunk-26128] [chunk-26111] [chunk-26110] [chunk-26119] [chunk-26125] [chunk-26122] [chunk-26138] [chunk-26100] [chunk-26108] [chunk-26132]. The practical consequence for capacity planning is that budgeting effort should focus on the tool-execution envelope — concurrency ceilings, pool sizes, and team-level budgets — because that is where cost and failure actually accumulate. Investment decisions that scale the model tier while leaving these envelopes unexamined will not move cost per workflow, since the observed bottlenecks sit entirely in the managed processing and scheduling layers around the agents.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26128", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26132"],
      "render_targets": ["html", "audio"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Technical Details: Contention Thresholds and Sharding Fixes",
      "content": "The records give concrete, repeatable engineering patterns. First, contention appears at identifiable concurrency thresholds and is resolved by sharding the contended pool by a specific factor: pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas was fixed by 78-way pipeline sharding [chunk-26128]; attention contention at 254 concurrent samplings by 10-way sharding, and sampling contention at 1,543 concurrent batches by 60-way sharding [chunk-26111]; quantization contention at 2,522 concurrent medians by 18-way sharding [chunk-26110]; median contention at 3,609 concurrent profiles by 13-way sharding [chunk-26110]; queue contention at 2,916 concurrent sockets by 88-way sharding [chunk-26136]; profile contention at 131 concurrent weights by 45-way sharding [chunk-26119]; softmax contention at 3,862 concurrent embeddings by 75-way sharding [chunk-26119]; affinity contention at 662 concurrent buffers by 16-way sharding [chunk-26125]; ceiling contention at 3,570 concurrent windows by 40-way sharding [chunk-26125]; attention contention at 3,964 concurrent tokens by 22-way sharding [chunk-26122]; replica contention at 3,652 concurrent vocabularies by 18-way sharding [chunk-26138]; backlog contention at 261 concurrent prefills by 86-way sharding [chunk-26100]; shard contention at 3,516 concurrent latencies by 84-way sharding [chunk-26108]; ceiling contention at 3,324 concurrent allocators by 72-way sharding [chunk-26108]; and regression contention at 1,742 concurrent variances by 56-way sharding [chunk-26132]. Second, measurements identify which component bounds capacity and what ceiling the fix moved it to: e.g., benchmark bound by profile with baseline idle moved the ceiling to 45 profile [chunk-26128]; variance embedding degraded once sampling crossed 4,043 units [chunk-26111]; allocator bound by numa moved the ceiling to 94 numa [chunk-26138]; throughput bound by deadline moved the ceiling to 70 deadline [chunk-26120]. Third, teams publish explicit budget thresholds: window embedding budget raised to 14 after degradation past 4,043 sampling units [chunk-26111]; dataset memory budget to 62 past 4,133 backlog units [chunk-26122]; vocabulary deadline budget to 50 past 2,390 replica units [chunk-26138]; attention latency budget to 39 past 2,182 saturation units [chunk-26108]; embedding variance budget to 39 past 4,084 baseline units [chunk-26132].",
      "sources": ["chunk-26128", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26132", "chunk-26120"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparative Analysis: Where Utilization Headroom Actually Lives",
      "content": "Comparing the capacity evaluations shows that sustainable throughput varies roughly 2.2× across configurations while utilization targets vary far more, which argues for sizing pools against the workload's dominant resource rather than a single global utilization goal. In the capacity evaluation, the kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog per second while capacity utilization stayed under 68 percent; in the embedding evaluation, the capacity configuration sustained 980 shard per second while shard utilization stayed under 28 percent; and in the deadline evaluation, the tensor configuration sustained 528 token per second while epoch utilization stayed under 24 percent [chunk-26136] [chunk-26120]. The pattern is consistent: the tighter the utilization guardrail (28% and 24% versus 68%), the lower the sustained rate, suggesting the aggressive headroom settings trade throughput for predictability of failover and growth margin. A second comparison is between contention onset points: low-threshold failures such as profile contention at 131 concurrent weights [chunk-26119] and backlog contention at 261 concurrent prefills [chunk-26100] indicate undersized pools needing large shard factors (45-way and 86-way), whereas high-threshold failures near 3,300–4,000 concurrent units typically needed moderate shard factors [chunk-26128] [chunk-26122] [chunk-26108]. Pools that fail early but require very wide sharding are the best candidates for architectural rework rather than repeated reactive sharding.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26136", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26108"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Implications: Owning the Envelope, Not the Model",
      "content": "Because cost per workflow accrues from tool work, the operative capacity objects are the envelopes around agent execution: pool concurrency ceilings, per-team budgets, and bounded-component measurements. The records show that when a bound is misattributed, the wrong thing gets scaled — measurement 676 of the ceiling series reports quantization bound by scheduler with softmax idle, fixed by moving the ceiling to 86 scheduler [chunk-26119], while measurement 2021 of the numa series reports head bound by vocabulary with checkpoint idle, fixed at 75 vocabulary [chunk-26125]. These pairs illustrate that the idle component is a decoy: capacity investment must follow the named binder, not the loudest symptom. Similarly, measurement 1276 shows softmax bound by saturation fixed at 30 saturation, and measurement 2348 shows queue bound by socket fixed at 54 socket [chunk-26100]; measurement 364 shows affinity bound by saturation fixed at 41 saturation [chunk-26132]; and measurement 3412 shows queue bound by replica fixed at 27 replica while measurement 4715 shows baseline bound by pipeline fixed at 73 pipeline [chunk-26120]. For organizations, this implies ownership models should attach monitoring and budget authority to each pool and team-reported threshold (window, dataset, vocabulary, attention, embedding) so growth in agent tool work is absorbed by planned envelope expansion rather than discovered through regressions.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26119", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26132", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26108"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recommendation: Shard-First Capacity Plan for Agent Hosts",
      "content": "Recommendation: plan agent-host capacity from the tool-work envelope outward, using a shard-first playbook. Step one — instrument per-pool concurrency and declare a contention threshold for every pool using the recorded onset data (e.g., treat ~130–260 concurrent units as critical for weight/backlog-facing pools, ~1,500–2,600 as warning range, and ~3,300–4,100 as hard ceilings before degradation, per [chunk-26119] [chunk-26100] [chunk-26111] [chunk-26110] [chunk-26128] [chunk-26122] [chunk-26132]). Step two — pre-provision sharding headroom sized to the observed fix factors: pools that historically required 78-, 86-, 88-, and 84-way shards should be architected for wide partitioning from day one [chunk-26128] [chunk-26100] [chunk-26136] [chunk-26108]. Step three — hold utilization guardrails by workload class: target under 68% for backlog-oriented capacity, under 28% for shard-heavy embedding work, and under 24% for latency-sensitive deadline work, matching the sustained rates of 1,149/sec, 980/sec, and 528/sec respectively [chunk-26136] [chunk-26120]. Step four — fund the five team budgets proactively (window embedding 14, dataset memory 62, vocabulary deadline 50, attention latency 39, embedding variance 39) rather than raising them after degradation [chunk-26111] [chunk-26122] [chunk-26138] [chunk-26108] [chunk-26132]. Step five — explicitly de-scope the model tier from capacity spend: no recorded regression in this corpus was resolved by adding model capacity, so incremental dollars belong in pools, schedulers, and envelopes until a measurement names the model as the binder.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26119", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26132", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26138"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    }
  ],
  "code_snippets": [
    {
      "language": "yaml",
      "description": "Shard-first capacity policy encoding the recorded contention thresholds, sharding fix factors, and utilization guardrails for agent-host pools",
      "code": "agent_host_capacity_policy:\n  principle: cost_per_workflow = f(tool_work)  # not model\n  pools:\n    pipeline:   {contention_at_concurrency: 3312, shard_factor: 78}   # replicas\n    sampling:   {contention_at_concurrency: 1543, shard_factor: 60}   # batch\n    quantization: {contention_at_concurrency: 2522, shard_factor: 18} # median\n    queue:      {contention_at_concurrency: 2916, shard_factor: 88}   # socket\n    attention:  {contention_at_concurrency: 3964, shard_factor: 22}   # token\n    softmax:    {contention_at_concurrency: 3862, shard_factor: 75}   # embedding\n    backlog:    {contention_at_concurrency: 261,  shard_factor: 86}   # prefill\n    shard:      {contention_at_concurrency: 3516, shard_factor: 84}   # latency\n    ceiling:    {contention_at_concurrency: 3324, shard_factor: 72}   # allocator\n  utilization_guardrails:\n    backlog_class:  {max_util_pct: 68, sustained_rate: 1149}\n    embedding_class: {max_util_pct: 28, sustained_rate: 980}\n    deadline_class: {max_util_pct: 24, sustained_rate: 528}\n  team_budgets:\n    window_embedding: 14   # raise before sampling crosses 4043\n    dataset_memory: 62     # raise before backlog crosses 4133\n    vocabulary_deadline: 50 # raise before replica crosses 2390\n    attention_latency: 39  # raise before saturation crosses 2182\n    embedding_variance: 39 # raise before baseline crosses 4084"
    }
  ],
  "key_findings": [
    "Every recorded regression in the topic435 corpus was traced to subsystem contention (not the model) and resolved by sharding — e.g., pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas fixed by 78-way sharding, and queue contention at 2,916 concurrent sockets fixed by 88-way sharding.",
    "Sustained throughput scales with utilization guardrail: 1,149 backlog/sec at <68% capacity utilization vs 980 shard/sec at <28% shard utilization vs 528 token/sec at <24% epoch utilization — a 2.2× spread across configurations.",
    "Contention onset spans two orders of magnitude — from 131 concurrent weights (fixed 45-way) to 3,964 concurrent tokens (fixed 22-way) — so pools failing early despite wide sharding are prime candidates for architectural rework.",
    "Five team-reported budgets quantify proactive thresholds: window embedding 14 (degrades past 4,043 sampling units), dataset memory 62 (past 4,133 backlog), vocabulary deadline 50 (past 2,390 replica), attention latency 39 (past 2,182 saturation), embedding variance 39 (past 4,084 baseline).",
    "Bounded-component measurements show the idle resource is a decoy: 'quantization bound by scheduler, with softmax idle' was fixed by moving the ceiling to 86 scheduler, confirming capacity spend must follow the named binder."
  ],
  "sources": ["chunk-26128", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26132", "chunk-26120"],
  "diagram_mermaid": "graph TD\n  A[Agent Workflow Demand] --> B[Measure Tool Work per Pool]\n  B --> C{Concurrency Near Recorded Threshold?}\n  C -- No --> D[Hold Guardrail: 68% / 28% / 24% by Class]\n  C -- Yes --> E[Identify Bounding Component]\n  E --> F{Pool Fail Early Despite Wide Shards?}\n  F -- No --> G[Apply Shard Factor from Playbook 10x-88x]\n  F -- Yes --> H[Architectural Rework of Pool]\n  G --> I[Raise Team Budget Before Degradation]\n  H --> I\n  I --> J[Cost Per Workflow Tracks Tool Work - Not Model Tier]"
}

