  "result": "Analysis of one week of service events for the payments platform (dataset L, 40,000,000 rows processed in 18,669.6 ms — 18,384.9 ms compute under network isolation) shows per-merchant volume and value concentrated at the top: the highest-value merchant keys are 1097 ($1,023,627.60), 689 ($1,017,842.60), 951 ($1,015,043.90), 1562 ($1,014,245.00), and 68 ($1,013,316.30), a spread of only ~$10.3k (~1.0%) between rank 1 and rank 5, indicating a flat head rather than a single dominant merchant. The hourly load profile peaks at hour 21 (9 PM), which is the scheduling and capacity-planning anchor for failover and burst-service recommendations. The value distribution has quantiles p50 = $83.93, p95 = $237.22, and p99 = $331.91, so the p99/p50 ratio is 3.96× while the mean of per-merchant key means is $100.014 — slightly above the median, confirming a right-skewed tail. Tail risk is bounded but real: 13 outlier merchants were flagged, and the maximum high-value share observed for any merchant is 0.0583 (5.83% of that merchant's events classified as high-value), well below thresholds that would indicate a compromised or misconfigured integration. Overall the week is predictable at the aggregate level (top-5 merchants each near $1.01–1.02M total value) with monitoring segments and routing-team attention best directed at the hour-21 peak and the 13 outlier keys.",
  "confidence": 0.72,
