{
  "title": "Final Brief: Lua 5.4.7 + SQLite Build Validation",
  "executive_summary": "Using only the build surfaced through the execution tool, the interpreter and its embedded database were taken through the full change cycle: construct the tree, run the test suite, remediate failures, and confirm. The build — lua-5.4.7+sqlite comprising 63 source files and 327,016 lines — compiled successfully in 34,263 ms, and both test suites executed in 1,185 ms under network-namespace isolation with 0 failures. No remediation was required because the suite passed clean on first confirmation. This brief records the plan of exactly three worker subtasks (build-and-test, fix-if-failing, confirm) and reports the resulting evidence.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Scope and Three-Subtask Worker Plan",
      "content": "The objective was strictly bounded: operate solely on the build exposed by the execution tool, with no external research, no upstream fetches, and no reference to prior knowledge of the Lua or SQLite codebases beyond what the tool returned. To honor that constraint while still completing a genuine change-validation cycle, the work was decomposed into EXACTLY three worker subtasks and no more. Subtask one — build the tree and run the test suite — invokes the execution tool once against the target artifact, capturing compile timing, file counts, and per-suite results. Subtask two — fix what fails — is conditional: it activates only if the suite reports non-zero failures, in which case the offending module is patched and the affected suite re-executed. Subtask three — confirm the tests — records the durable evidence trail via the record-keeping tool so the final state (pass/fail, timings, isolation mode) is auditable rather than ephemeral. Deliberately capping the plan at three subtasks prevents runaway iteration: each tool call is single-shot per subtask, so the report reflects one honest measurement pass instead of a tuned sequence. The retrieved context reinforces this framing — validation platforms differ across agent schedulers in how they accumulate concurrent monitoring data, and predictable failover procedures matter most when ownership of the envelope is compressed into a small number of managed processing steps. Our three-step plan is precisely such a compressed envelope.",
      "sources": [],
      "render_targets": ["html", "audio"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Build Results: Tree Construction and Test Execution",
      "content": "The execution tool reported a complete, self-contained result for the combined artifact. The target is lua-5.4.7+sqlite — the Lua 5.4.7 interpreter built together with its embedded SQLite database layer — spanning 63 source files and 327,016 lines of code. Full-tree construction completed in 34,263 ms (roughly 34.3 seconds), which is consistent with a modest C codebase compiled without distributed caching but including both the interpreter core and the bundled database engine in a single pass. Immediately following compilation, the test harness ran 2 suites in 1,185 ms total — approximately 29x faster than the build itself — and returned 0 failures. Two details deserve emphasis. First, the suites executed under network-namespace isolation (netns), meaning the embedded database tests could not silently depend on external services or shared sockets; all I/O was confined to the local namespace, which materially raises confidence that the 0-failure result is reproducible. Second, the ratio of test time to build time (1,185 ms vs 34,263 ms) shows the suite is a fast feedback loop relative to compilation, so the conditional 'fix what fails' subtask would have been cheap to iterate had any test broken. Because the suite passed clean, subtask two resolved to a no-op patch cycle: nothing failed, therefore nothing needed fixing, and the confirmation step simply attests to the green state rather than to a repaired one.",
      "sources": [],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Change Cycle Integrity: Fix-If-Failing and Confirmation",
      "content": "A validation brief is only as strong as its failure path, so the second and third subtasks were designed around what happens when the suite is red. Had any of the 2 suites reported failures, the fix subtask would have localized the fault to either the interpreter side (Lua 5.4.7 core: parser, VM, garbage collector) or the embedded-database side (SQLite integration within the same 63-file tree), applied the minimal patch, and re-run only the affected suite inside the same netns isolation to avoid environment drift between runs. The observed outcome made that branch unnecessary: 0 failures on the first execution means the change is confirmed as-built, with no delta between the delivered tree and the validated tree. The third subtask then stored the result durably through the record-keeping tool, retrieving supporting context about how envelopes are handled across investment-grade monitoring segments — specifically that growth in bandwidth and infrastructure margins accumulates while agents are processing concurrent queries, and that findings differ across platforms depending on who owns the compressed validation procedures. Translated to engineering terms: the value of this run lies not in the single green checkmark but in the fact that the evidence (63 files, 327,016 lines, 34,263 ms build, 1,185 ms tests, 0 failures, netns isolation) is now attached to a durable record that any later scheduler can replay. Failover predictability depends on exactly this property — a successor agent can trust the recorded state without re-paying the 34-second build.",
      "sources": [],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Implications and Recommendations",
      "content": "Three recommendations follow directly from the evidence. (1) Treat this build as the canonical baseline: lua-5.4.7+sqlite compiles cleanly end-to-end in 34,263 ms and passes 2/2 suites in 1,185 ms with zero failures under network-namespace isolation, so any future change should be diffed against these exact numbers; a build time drifting far above ~34 s or a suite time straying far above ~1.2 s signals environmental contamination even before a test formally fails. (2) Preserve netns isolation in every subsequent run of the embedded-database suites — the 0-failure result is only meaningful because external dependencies were cut off; running the same 63-file, 327,016-line tree against a shared network could mask real integration defects. (3) Keep the three-subtask cap as policy for comparable validations: one execute call, one conditional fix pass, one record call. The retrieved context notes that agent schedulers running concurrent monitoring differ in how they accumulate and compress processing findings across segments, and unbounded retry loops amplify that variance; a fixed three-step envelope makes each validation's cost and evidence footprint predictable regardless of which platform executes it. If a future run does go red, the recommended remediation order is interpreter-core checks first (the larger surface of the combined tree), then SQLite binding-layer checks, re-running only the failed suite to keep the feedback loop near its observed 1,185 ms.",
      "sources": [],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    }
  ],
  "code_snippets": [
    {
      "language": "text",
      "description": "Execution-tool job specification and verbatim result for the build-and-test subtask",
      "code": "task: \"take the interpreter and its embedded database through the change: build the tree and run the test suite\"\nsize: build\n\nEXECUTION COMPLETE:\n  artifact   : lua-5.4.7+sqlite\n  tree       : 63 source files / 327,016 lines\n  build time : 34,263 ms\n  test suites: 2 run in 1,185 ms\n  failures   : 0\n  isolation  : network namespace (netns)"
    }
  ],
  "key_findings": [
    "lua-5.4.7+sqlite builds fully from 63 source files (327,016 lines) in 34,263 ms using only the execution-tool-provided build, with no external research",
    "Both test suites (2/2) pass in 1,185 ms under network-namespace isolation with 0 failures — the fix subtask resolved to a verified no-op",
    "Test feedback is ~29x faster than compilation (1,185 ms vs 34,263 ms), making the conditional repair loop cheap enough to fit within the strict three-subtask budget",
    "All evidence (file counts, timings, isolation mode, 0 failures) was stored durably via the record tool in a single call, so downstream schedulers can trust the green state without re-running the 34-second build"
  ],
  "sources": [],
  "diagram_mermaid": "graph TD\n  A[Subtask 1: Execute build] --> B[lua-5.4.7+sqlite tree\\n63 files / 327,016 lines]\n  B --> C[Compile in 34,263 ms]\n  C --> D[Run 2 test suites\\nnetns isolation, 1,185 ms]\n  D --> E{Failures > 0?}\n  E -- no --> F[Subtask 2: Fix pass = no-op]\n  E -- yes --> G[Patch interpreter or SQLite layer\\nre-run failed suite]\n  G --> F\n  F --> H[Subtask 3: Record durable evidence]\n  H --> I[Confirmed: 0 failures\\nbaseline locked]"
}

