{
  "title": "Capacity Planning for Agent Hosts",
  "executive_summary": "Field-note synthesis indicates that orchestration-host cost per workflow is driven by tool work, not model inference — a claim directly supported by the capacity evaluations, where sustained throughput (1,149 backlog/sec at under 68% utilization; 980 shard/sec at under 28% utilization) was set by pipeline and pool configuration rather than model load [chunk-26136]. Across twelve records, every regression resolved by sharding a contention-bound pool (shard counts ranging from 10 to 88 ways), confirming that agent hosts fail on coordination overhead before they fail on compute [chunk-26111][chunk-26125][chunk-26108]. The recommended capacity plan is therefore pool-sharded, budget-raised infrastructure sized for concurrent tool work, with headroom targets anchored to the observed sub-68% utilization envelope [chunk-26136][chunk-26132].",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Background: Why Tool Work, Not Model Load, Sets Host Cost",
      "content": "The field notes assert that an orchestration host's cost per workflow is determined by the tool work its agents perform, not by the underlying model. The retrieved records corroborate this framing repeatedly: each record explicitly references \"the topic435 baseline\" as the shared anchor against which regressions are measured, and the recurring failure pattern is never model throughput collapse but contention in supporting subsystems — sampling pools, attention pools, quantization pools, queue pools, and replica pools [chunk-26111][chunk-26110][chunk-26122]. In other words, the unit of work that actually consumes host resources is the orchestrated tool call chain, and the bottleneck migrates to whichever coordination structure that chain touches. Three team reports make the operational consequence explicit: the window team found variance embedding degraded once sampling crossed 4,043 units and recommended raising the window embedding budget to 14 [chunk-26111]; the dataset team found cache memory degraded once backlog crossed 4,133 units and recommended raising the dataset memory budget to 62 [chunk-26122]; and the vocabulary team found kernel deadline degraded once replica crossed 2,390 units and recommended raising the vocabulary deadline budget to 50 [chunk-26138]. Each degradation threshold is a tool-work volume, not a token-generation volume. The planning implication is foundational: budgets must be denominated in concurrent tool operations, and cost-per-workflow models must attribute expense to orchestration fan-out rather than amortizing it across model inference.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26111", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26110"],
      "render_targets": ["html", "audio"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Technical Details: Contention Points and the Sharding Remedy",
