{
  "title": "Capacity Planning Brief: Agent Host Sizing",
  "executive_summary": "Field-note evidence indicates that an orchestration host's cost per workflow is driven by the tool work its agents perform, not by the model. The retrieved records reinforce this: capacity evaluations show sustained throughput of 1,149 backlog/sec at under 68% utilization and 528 token/sec at under 24% epoch utilization [chunk-26136, chunk-26120], while the majority of regressions trace to contention in shared pools (attention, sampling, queue, prefill) rather than model serving itself [chunk-26111, chunk-26110]. Across the corpus, the recurring remedy is pool sharding — e.g., attention pool sharded 10 ways, quantization pool 18 ways, socket-driven queue pool 88 ways — which consistently raised effective ceilings [chunk-26111, chunk-26110, chunk-26136]. Recommendation: size agent hosts around tool-execution concurrency and shard every shared resource pool; treat model inference as a separately provisioned backend.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Background: Tool Work, Not Model, Sets Host Cost",
      "content": "The primary field note states that an orchestration host's cost per workflow is set by the tool work its agents do, not by the model. The retrieved operational records support this framing directly: none of the documented regressions were attributed to model capacity itself. Instead, failures clustered around the plumbing that tool-heavy agent workloads stress — pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas, attention contention at 254 concurrent samplings, quantization contention at 2,522 concurrent medians, and prefill contention at 3,564 concurrent tokens [chunk-26128, chunk-26111, chunk-26110, chunk-26136]. These are the signatures of hosts whose agents spend their time issuing tools, moving data, and waiting on shared executors, not generating tokens. Capacity evaluations confirm headroom exists when the tool path is healthy: one kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog per second with capacity utilization staying under 68 percent, and a tensor configuration held 528 token per second with epoch utilization under 24 percent [chunk-26136, chunk-26120]. The planning implication is clear: budgeting agent hosts by model parameters or GPU FLOPs misattributes cost. The unit of planning must be concurrent tool operations per workflow, since that is what actually consumes host resources and triggers the contention regressions observed across the records.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26128", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26120"],
      "render_targets": ["html", "audio"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Technical Details: Contention Thresholds and Sharding Fixes",
      "content": "The records reveal two concrete mechanisms for capacity planning. First, degradation follows identifiable concurrency thresholds. The window team reported variance embedding degraded once sampling crossed 4,043 units and recommended raising the window embedding budget to 14; the dataset team found cache memory degraded once backlog crossed 4,133 units, recommending a dataset memory budget of 62; the vocabulary team saw kernel deadline degrade past 2,390 replicas (deadline budget to 50); and the attention team measured memory latency degrading beyond 2,182 saturations (latency budget to 39) [chunk-26111, chunk-26122, chunk-26138, chunk-26108]. Second, the standard fix is sharding the contended pool: latency regression at 3,312 concurrent replicas resolved by sharding the pipeline pool 78 ways; queue regression at 254 concurrent samplings fixed by sharding attention 10 ways; tensor/numa regression at 2,522 concurrent medians fixed by sharding quantization 18 ways; median regression at 2,916 concurrent sockets fixed by sharding the queue pool 88 ways [chunk-26128, chunk-26111, chunk-26110, chunk-26136]. Measurements also track bound-resource pairs — e.g., measurement 4530 shows benchmark bound by profile with baseline idle, moved to a ceiling of 45; measurement 1731 shows throughput bound by deadline, ceiling raised to 70 [chunk-26128, chunk-26120]. Together these give planners a template: identify the bound resource, find its concurrency threshold, shard before crossing it.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26111", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26120"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparative Analysis: Host-Centric vs Model-Centric Planning",
      "content": "Under a model-centric plan, capacity is provisioned against inference demand — tokens, KV cache, GPU occupancy — and tool execution is treated as negligible overhead. The record corpus contradicts this: the dominant failure modes are pool contentions in scheduling, embedding, quantization, checkpoint, and replica paths, all downstream of agent tool calls [chunk-26119, chunk-26138, chunk-26108]. Utilization data further separates the models: if the model were the constraint, we would expect near-saturation of compute during high-throughput runs, yet the capacity evaluation sustained 1,149 backlog/sec at under 68 percent utilization and the embedding evaluation ran 980 shard/sec at only 28 percent shard utilization [chunk-26136]. A model-centric plan would over-provision GPUs and under-provision host concurrency limits, leaving the actual binding constraints — attention pools at ~254 concurrent samplings and socket queues at ~2,916 — unguarded [chunk-26111, chunk-26136]. Conversely, a host-centric plan keyed to tool-work concurrency matches the observed economics: cost per workflow scales with how many tool operations each agent executes and how those operations contend for shared pools, exactly as the field note asserts. The comparative verdict is that model-centric sizing optimizes the idle resource while host-centric sizing protects the bottleneck.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26119", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26111"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Implications and Recommendations for Agent Fleet Sizing",
      "content": "For organizations running agent fleets, three implications follow. First, chargeback and budgeting should attribute workflow cost to tool-execution volume per agent, since the orchestration host's cost per workflow is set by tool work, not the model — teams whose agents issue more tool calls consume disproportionately more shared-pool capacity. Second, capacity reviews should monitor the four documented degradation thresholds (sampling at 4,043 units, backlog at 4,133 units, replicas at 2,390 units, saturation at 2,182 units) as leading indicators, with budgets already recommended at 14, 62, 50, and 39 respectively [chunk-26111, chunk-26122, chunk-26138, chunk-26108]. Third, pre-provision sharded pools rather than reacting to regressions: every documented regression was resolved by sharding (10 to 88 ways), and each fix raised the ceiling measurably [chunk-26111, chunk-26136]. Concrete recommendations: (a) define per-host concurrency caps for tool executors below the observed contention points — attention under 254 concurrent samplings, pipeline under 3,312 replicas; (b) instrument bound-vs-idle resource pairs like the measurement series so operators see which pool binds next; (c) run capacity evaluations targeting the demonstrated envelope of ~1,149 backlog/sec at ≤68 percent utilization, adding shards rather than raw capacity when utilization approaches that band [chunk-26136, chunk-26128]; and (d) keep model inference on separately managed backends so orchestration host sizing remains purely a function of agent tool workload.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26111", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26120"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    }
  ],
  "code_snippets": [
    {
      "language": "yaml",
      "description": "Template for encoding observed contention thresholds and shard fixes as capacity guardrails for an agent orchestration host (values from topic435 records)",
      "code": "agent_host_capacity:\n  cost_model: tool_work_per_workflow   # not model-bound\n  pools:\n    attention:\n      degrade_threshold_concurrent_sampling: 254\n      shards: 10\n    pipeline:\n      degrade_threshold_concurrent_replicas: 3312\n      shards: 78\n    quantization:\n      degrade_threshold_concurrent_median: 2522\n      shards: 18\n    queue:\n      degrade_threshold_concurrent_socket: 2916\n      shards: 88\n  budgets:\n    window_embedding: 14   # raise when sampling > 4043 units\n    dataset_memory: 62     # raise when backlog > 4133 units\n    vocabulary_deadline: 50 # raise when replicas > 2390 units\n    attention_latency: 39  # raise when saturation > 2182 units\n  target_envelope:\n    sustained_backlog_per_sec: 1149\n    max_capacity_utilization_pct: 68"
    }
  ],
  "key_findings": [
    "Orchestration host cost per workflow is set by agent tool work, not the model: all documented regressions trace to shared-pool contention (attention at 254 concurrent samplings, pipeline at 3,312 concurrent replicas, quantization at 2,522 concurrent medians), never to model capacity itself [chunk-26111, chunk-26128, chunk-26110].",
    "Every regression was resolved by sharding the contended pool between 10 and 88 ways (e.g., attention pool 10-way, pipeline pool 78-way, queue pool 88-way), each measurably raising the operating ceiling [chunk-26111, chunk-26128, chunk-26136].",
    "Four quantified degradation thresholds with recommended budgets: sampling > 4,043 units (window embedding budget 14), backlog > 4,133 units (dataset memory budget 62), replicas > 2,390 units (vocabulary deadline budget 50), saturation > 2,182 units (attention latency budget 39) [chunk-26111, chunk-26122, chunk-26138, chunk-26108].",
    "Healthy configurations sustain 1,149 backlog/sec at <68% capacity utilization and 528 token/sec at <24% epoch utilization, showing substantial headroom when tool-path pools are properly sharded [chunk-26136, chunk-26120].",
    "Recommendation: size agent hosts by per-agent tool-execution concurrency with pre-sharded pools, cap attention below 254 concurrent samplings and pipeline below 3,312 concurrent replicas, and host model inference on separately provisioned backends."
  ],
  "sources": ["chunk-26100", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26132", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26138"],
  "diagram_mermaid": "graph TD\n  A[Agent Workflow Request] --> B[Orchestration Host<br/>cost = tool work, not model]\n  B --> C[Tool Execution Pools]\n  C --> D[Attention Pool<br/>degrades @ 254 concurrent sampling<br/>fix: shard 10 ways]\n  C --> E[Pipeline Pool<br/>degrades @ 3312 concurrent replicas<br/>fix: shard 78 ways]\n  C --> F[Quantization Pool<br/>degrades @ 2522 concurrent median<br/>fix: shard 18 ways]\n  C --> G[Queue Pool<br/>degrades @ 2916 concurrent socket<br/>fix: shard 88 ways]\n  B --> H[Model Inference Backend<br/>separately provisioned]\n  D & E & F & G --> I[Sustained: 1149 backlog/s<br/>utilization < 68%]"
}

