{
  "title": "Capacity Planning Brief: Agent Hosts",
  "executive_summary": "Per the field notes, an orchestration host's cost per workflow is set by the tool work its agents do, not by the model — so capacity planning for agent hosts should be driven by tool-execution load (concurrency, contention, and pool sizing) rather than by model inference budgets. The retrieved operations records reinforce this: recurring regressions were traced to contention in shared pools at specific concurrency thresholds (e.g., attention contention at 3,964 concurrent tokens [chunk-26122], softmax contention at 3,862 concurrent embeddings [chunk-26119]) and were resolved by sharding those pools between 10 and 88 ways [chunk-26111, chunk-26136]. Capacity evaluations show that sustained throughput of ~1,149 backlog/s was achievable with utilization held under 68 percent [chunk-26136], indicating headroom-based planning works. Recommendation: plan agent host capacity around measured tool-work concurrency, shard every shared resource pool per workload class, and enforce utilization ceilings well below saturation.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Core Principle: Tool Work Drives Host Cost, Not the Model",
      "content": "Field note (1) states plainly: an orchestration host's cost per workflow is set by the tool work its agents do, not by the model. This reframes capacity planning for agent hosts. The orchestration machine is not a GPU serving box; it is a coordination plane whose consumption scales with the number, duration, and concurrency of tool invocations each agent makes — shell commands, retrieval queries, API calls, file operations, and subprocess management. If a workflow spends its wall-clock and CPU budget waiting on and managing dozens of tool calls, then adding model-side capacity to the host does nothing for throughput or cost, while under-provisioning the tool-execution path (process slots, sockets, connection pools, scheduler queues) causes exactly the failure patterns visible in the operations records. The retrieved records are dominated by contention events on shared infrastructure pools rather than model-bound limits: latency regression traced to pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas [chunk-26128], queue regression traced to attention contention at 254 concurrent samplings [chunk-26111], and socket regressions traced to profile and affinity contention [chunk-26119, chunk-26125]. Every one of these is a tool-plane or coordination-plane resource, not a model resource. The planning implication is direct: forecast cost per workflow as (average tool calls per workflow) x (average concurrent occupancy per call) x (host resource-second rates), and treat model token cost as a separate line item owned by the inference platform, not the orchestration host.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26128", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26125"],
      "render_targets": ["html", "audio"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Evidence From the Field: Contention Thresholds and Shard Fixes",
      "content": "The operations records give concrete, repeatable evidence for how agent-host capacity actually fails: gradual degradation is rare, while threshold-triggered contention is the norm. Regression after regression was traced to a single shared pool saturating past a concurrency count — attention contention at 3,964 concurrent tokens resolved by sharding the attention pool 22 ways [chunk-26122]; softmax contention at 3,862 concurrent embeddings resolved by sharding the softmax pool 75 ways [chunk-26119]; ceiling contention at 3,570 concurrent windows resolved by sharding the ceiling pool 40 ways [chunk-26125]; replica contention at 3,652 concurrent vocabulary units resolved by sharding the replica pool 18 ways [chunk-26138]; and queue contention at 2,916 concurrent sockets resolved by sharding the queue pool 88 ways [chunk-26136]. The shard factors vary widely (10 to 88 ways), which tells us there is no universal setting — each pool must be sized to its own contention threshold, discovered empirically. Team reports corroborate the threshold pattern: variance embedding degraded once sampling crossed 4,043 units with a recommended window embedding budget raise to 14 [chunk-26111]; cache memory degraded once backlog crossed 4,133 units with a dataset memory budget raise to 62 [chunk-26122]; kernel deadline degraded once replica crossed 2,390 units with a vocabulary deadline budget raise to 50 [chunk-26138]; and memory latency degraded once saturation crossed 2,182 units with an attention latency budget raise to 39 [chunk-26108]. For capacity planning, these crossing points are the real planning variables: identify the first-degrading resource, record its concurrency threshold, and provision below it with explicit budget raises as the modeled growth lever.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26122", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26108"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "What Healthy Capacity Looks Like: Evaluation Baselines",
      "content": "Two evaluation records define what a properly sized configuration looks like in practice, and both emphasize headroom over maximization. In the capacity evaluation, the kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog per second while capacity utilization stayed under 68 percent [chunk-26136]. In the same record set, the embedding evaluation's capacity configuration sustained 980 shards per second while shard utilization stayed under 28 percent [chunk-26136], and the deadline evaluation's tensor configuration sustained 528 tokens per second with epoch utilization under 24 percent [chunk-26120]. The consistent story: healthy agent-host operation occurs at modest utilization percentages, not near-saturation. This matches the contention evidence — since failures arrive as cliff-edge regressions once a pool crosses its concurrency threshold, running hot guarantees that ordinary burstiness in tool-call fan-out will push individual pools over their cliffs even when aggregate utilization looks acceptable. The measurement series also show that fixes rebind the constraint rather than eliminate it: after resolving one bottleneck, the ceiling moved to whichever resource was next (e.g., ceilings moving to 45 profile [chunk-26128], 22 queue [chunk-26110], 86 scheduler [chunk-26119], 70 deadline and 27 replica and 73 pipeline in a single record [chunk-26120]). Capacity planning must therefore be iterative: each shard-and-fix cycle exposes the next binding constraint, so plans should budget for continuous rebinding rather than a one-time sizing exercise.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26136", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26119"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recommendation and Operating Procedure",
      "content": "Recommendation: size agent orchestration hosts against projected tool-work concurrency, not model demand, and manage them as a set of independently sharded resource pools with published degradation thresholds. Concretely: (1) Instrument per-pool concurrency for every tool-plane resource — process/socket pools, queue pools, attention/embedding-equivalent worker pools — and alert at 60-65 percent of the observed contention cliff, consistent with the sub-68-percent utilization envelope of the healthy capacity evaluation [chunk-26136]. (2) When a regression appears, trace it to the contended pool and shard it using the established remedy pattern; documented shard factors range from 10 ways for attention pools at low concurrency [chunk-26111] up to 88 ways for queue pools at 2,916 concurrent sockets [chunk-26136], so calibrate shard count to the threshold, not to convention. (3) Codify team-reported budget crossings (4,043 sampling units, 4,133 backlog units, 2,390 replica units, 2,182 saturation units) into the capacity model as hard planning ceilings with pre-approved budget raises (14, 62, 50, 39 respectively) [chunk-26111, chunk-26122, chunk-26138, chunk-26108]. (4) After every fix, re-measure to find the newly bound resource, since the records consistently show the ceiling relocating to the next constraint [chunk-26120]. (5) Report cost per workflow as tool-seconds consumed per completed workflow, keeping model token spend attributed separately to the inference platform. This procedure converts the field-note principle — tool work sets host cost — into an operational loop that prevents the contention cliffs from being discovered by production traffic.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26136", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26120"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    }
  ],
