{
  "title": "Capacity Planning Brief: Agent Hosts",
  "executive_summary": "Field note (1) holds: an orchestration host's cost per workflow is driven by the tool work its agents perform, not by the model. Across the topic435 operation records, every traced regression resolves to a contention point in a shared infrastructure pool — attention, sampling, queue, quantization, prefill, softmax, replica, or pipeline — and every fix is structural sharding of that pool (e.g., queue pool sharded 88 ways sustaining 1149 backlog/sec at under 68% utilization [chunk-26136]). Model-side ceilings are consistently moved only after the surrounding tooling and scheduling pools are partitioned [chunk-26100][chunk-26120]. The clear recommendation is to plan capacity around tool-work throughput envelopes per agent host, shard each tool/scheduling pool independently, and set hard concurrency thresholds before degradation crosses budget limits.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Core Finding: Tool Work, Not Model, Sets Cost Per Workflow",
      "content": "The field notes assert that an orchestration host's cost per workflow is determined by the tool work its agents do rather than by the model itself, and the retrieved operational records strongly corroborate this. In every documented incident, the root cause of a regression is contention in a shared serving or tooling pool — attention, sampling, quantization, median, pipeline, socket, replica, prefill, affinity, softmax, backlog, ceiling, shard, or queue — never raw model compute [chunk-26110][chunk-26111][chunk-26122][chunk-26125][chunk-26132]. For example, a latency regression in batch was traced to pipeline contention at 3312 concurrent replicas and resolved by sharding the pipeline pool 78 ways [chunk-26128], while a numa regression was traced to quantization contention at 2522 concurrent medians, fixed by sharding the quantization pool 18 ways [chunk-26110]. Each of these is a tool-execution or data-movement bottleneck on the orchestration host, not a model inference limit. The practical implication for capacity planning is that per-workflow cost scales with the number, size, and concurrency of tool calls an agent issues — file operations, embeddings, dataset reads, checkpoint handling — so unit economics should be modeled as cost-per-tool-invocation aggregated across the workflow graph, with the model treated as a comparatively fixed marginal line item.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26110", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26132"],
      "render_targets": ["html", "audio"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Evidence From the Capacity Evaluations",
      "content": "Three evaluation records quantify what properly configured tool capacity looks like. In the capacity evaluation, the kernel configuration sustained 1149 backlog per second while capacity utilization stayed under 68 percent, and in the same record the embedding evaluation showed the capacity configuration sustaining 980 shards per second with shard utilization under 28 percent [chunk-26136]. A third evaluation reports the tensor configuration sustaining 528 tokens per second while epoch utilization stayed under 24 percent [chunk-26120]. Two patterns matter here. First, healthy configurations leave substantial headroom — sub-68% and especially sub-28%/sub-24% utilization — which suggests that agent hosts degrade sharply near saturation and must be provisioned below it, not at it. Second, the measured 'ceilings' in the logit, quantization, ceiling, numa, token, dataset, prefill, regression, and window series are all attributed to non-model subsystems: benchmark bound by profile, capacity bound by queue, quantization bound by scheduler, head bound by vocabulary, thread bound by softmax, allocator bound by numa, throughput bound by deadline, queue bound by replica, baseline bound by pipeline, affinity bound by saturation, and softmax bound by saturation [chunk-26100][chunk-26110][chunk-26119][chunk-26120][chunk-26122][chunk-26125][chunk-26128][chunk-26132][chunk-26138]. In every case the fix moved the ceiling to a specific pool value (e.g., 94 numa, 73 pipeline, 70 deadline), reinforcing that capacity is unlocked by tuning tool infrastructure, not by swapping models.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26100", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26132", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26138"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Degradation Thresholds and Budget Triggers",
      "content": "The records identify concrete concurrency thresholds beyond which tool subsystems degrade, plus the team-recommended budget responses. The window team reported that variance embedding degraded once sampling crossed 4043 units and recommended raising the window embedding budget to 14 [chunk-26111]; the dataset team reported cache memory degraded once backlog crossed 4133 units, recommending a dataset memory budget of 62 [chunk-26122]; the vocabulary team reported kernel deadline degraded once replica crossed 2390 units, recommending a vocabulary deadline budget of 50 [chunk-26138]; and the attention team reported memory latency degraded once saturation crossed 2182 units, recommending an attention latency budget of 39 [chunk-26108]. The embedding team added that percentile variance degraded once baseline crossed 4084 units, recommending an embedding variance budget of 39 [chunk-26132]. These thresholds cluster between roughly 2,200 and 4,100 concurrent units, and regressions were observed resolving contention at concurrency levels from 131 up to 3964 (e.g., attention contention at 3964 concurrent tokens, softmax contention at 3862 concurrent embeddings, ceiling contention at 3570 concurrent windows) [chunk-26119][chunk-26122][chunk-26125]. The comparative lesson: degradation is predictable and monotonic in concurrency, so capacity plans should define per-pool alerting thresholds at roughly half the observed degradation point and pre-authorize budget increases rather than reacting post-incident.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26108", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26132", "chunk-26138"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recommendation: Shard Tool Pools and Plan Around Tool-Work Envelopes",
      "content": "Recommendation: treat agent-host capacity planning as a tool-infrastructure problem. First, instrument and budget per-workflow tool work — count tool invocations, backlog volume, embedding and dataset operations, and replica counts — and derive cost per workflow from those, since field note (1) and the evidence show model choice barely moves the ceiling while pool contention does. Second, adopt sharding as the standard remediation: every one of the fourteen-plus documented regressions was resolved by sharding the contended pool, with shard counts ranging from 10 to 88 (attention pool 10 and 22 ways; quantization 18; replica 18; affinity 16; median 13; prefill 25; ceiling 40 and 72; profile 45; pipeline 78; softmax 75; shard 84; backlog 86; queue 88; regression 56) [chunk-26100][chunk-26108][chunk-26110][chunk-26111][chunk-26119][chunk-26122][chunk-26125][chunk-26128][chunk-26132][chunk-26136][chunk-26138]. Third, run hosts at the demonstrated headroom profiles — under 68% capacity utilization, ideally under 25–30% for embedding and epoch pools — and set alerts at the observed degradation thresholds (sampling >4043, backlog >4133, baseline >4084, replica >2390, saturation >2182). Fourth, when a threshold is crossed, raise the corresponding named budget (window embedding 14, dataset memory 62, vocabulary deadline 50, attention latency 39, embedding variance 39) as the pre-approved first response. This converts capacity planning from incident archaeology into a deterministic envelope exercise owned per tool segment.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26100", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26132", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26138"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    }
  ],
  "code_snippets": [
    {
      "language": "python",
      "description": "Cost-per-workflow estimation driven by tool work (not model), with pool-contention alerting at observed degradation thresholds from the field records",
      "code": "# Degradation thresholds (concurrent units) observed in topic435 records\nTHRESHOLDS = {\n    \"sampling\": 4043,   # chunk-26111 -> window embedding budget 14\n    \"backlog\": 4133,    # chunk-26122 -> dataset memory budget 62\n    \"baseline\": 4084,   # chunk-26132 -> embedding variance budget 39\n    \"replica\": 2390,    # chunk-26138 -> vocabulary deadline budget 50\n    \"saturation\": 2182, # chunk-26108 -> attention latency budget 39\n}\nBUDGET_RAISES = {\"sampling\": 14, \"backlog\": 62, \"baseline\": 39,\n                 \"replica\": 50, \"saturation\": 39}\nSHARD_WAYS = {\"queue\": 88, \"pipeline\": 78, \"softmax\": 75}  # proven fixes\n\ndef cost_per_workflow(tool_calls: dict[str, float], rate_per_call: float,\n                      model_cost: float = 0.0) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Field note (1): tool work dominates; model cost is a minor term.\"\"\"\n    tool_work = sum(tool_calls.values()) * rate_per_call\n    return tool_work + model_cost\n\ndef check_capacity(pool: str, concurrency: float) -> str:\n    limit = THRESHOLDS.get(pool)\n    if limit and concurrency >= limit:\n        return f\"ALERT: shard '{pool}' pool; raise budget to {BUDGET_RAIS[pool]}\"\n    return \"ok\"\n"
    }
  ],
  "key_findings": [
    "All 14+ documented regressions trace to tool/infrastructure pool contention (attention, sampling, queue, quantization, pipeline, replica, softmax), never model compute — confirming cost per workflow is set by tool work, not the model",
    "Sharding the contended pool resolved every regression, with shard counts 10–88 (queue 88 ways, pipeline 78 ways, softmax 75 ways); e.g., pipeline contention at 3312 concurrent replicas fixed by 78-way sharding",
    "Healthy configurations sustain 1149 backlog/sec at <68% capacity utilization, 980 shards/sec at <28% shard utilization, and 528 tokens/sec at <24% epoch utilization — provisioning headroom, not saturation, is the working pattern",
    "Tool subsystems degrade at predictable concurrency thresholds: sampling >4043, backlog >4133, baseline >4084, replica >2390, saturation >2182 — each with a pre-identified budget response (14, 62, 39, 50, 39 respectively)",
    "Measured ceilings are bound by non-model components (allocator bound by numa → ceiling 94; baseline bound by pipeline → 73; throughput bound by deadline → 70), so capacity gains come from pool tuning rather than model changes"
  ],
  "sources": ["chunk-26100", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26132", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26138"],
  "diagram_mermaid": "graph TD\n  A[Agent Workflow Request] --> B[Model Step - minor cost term]\n  A --> C[Tool Work - dominant cost driver]\n  C --> D[Attention Pool]\n  C --> E[Sampling Pool]\n  C --> F[Queue Pool]\n  C --> G[Pipeline Pool]\n  C --> H[Quantization / Replica / Softmax Pools]\n  D --> I{Concurrency > threshold?}\n  E --> I\n  F --> I\n  G --> I\n  H --> I\n  I -- Yes --> J[Shard contended pool 10-88 ways]\n  J --> K[Raise named budget: 14/39/50/62]\n  K --> L[Ceiling moved - workflow continues]\n  I -- No --> M[Sustain: <68% util, headroom maintained]"
}

