{
  "title": "Build Brief: lua-5.4.7+sqlite Tree and Tests",
  "executive_summary": "The change was taken through the interpreter and its embedded database using only the build surfaced by the execution tool: a lua-5.4.7+sqlite tree of 63 source files totaling 327,016 lines, compiled in 30,170 ms. The test suite executed 2 suites in 1,099 ms with 0 failures under network-namespace isolation (netns). No source defects were exposed by the run, so no fixes were required and the confirmation step reduces to re-affirming the green result rather than a repair-and-rerest cycle. The plan was held to exactly three worker subtasks — research (build the tree), execution (run the tests), and record/validation (synthesize findings) — with no additional research permitted or performed.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Scope and Constraints of the Change",
      "content": "This brief covers a bounded engineering task: take the Lua 5.4.7 interpreter and its embedded SQLite database through a change cycle using ONLY the build made available through the execution tool. The instruction explicitly forbade further research, which shaped every downstream decision — no upstream changelog mining, no CVE cross-referencing, no comparison against independently fetched release tarballs. The artifact under test is a single composite build, lua-5.4.7+sqlite, comprising 63 source files and 327,016 lines. That line count is dominated by SQLite's amalgamation, which is expected and not itself a signal of scope creep; the interpreter core contributes a comparatively small fraction of the total. The work was decomposed into exactly three worker subtasks and deliberately capped there: (1) research/build the tree, (2) execute the test suite over that tree, and (3) record the outcome and synthesize it into this report. Because the cap was fixed at three, any hypothetical fourth subtask — deeper root-cause analysis, performance profiling, or an independent verification pass — was intentionally excluded rather than deferred. This is a meaningful constraint to state plainly: the confidence expressed later in this brief is confidence within a three-subtask envelope, not the confidence that would come from repeated or adversarial validation.",
      "sources": [],
      "render_targets": ["html", "audio"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Build and Test Results",
      "content": "The execution tool reported a complete compile of the lua-5.4.7+sqlite tree in 30,170 ms — roughly 30.2 seconds for 63 source files across 327,016 lines, which works out to about 10.8 thousand lines compiled per second. For a mixed C tree containing a large single-translation-unit dependency, that throughput is unremarkable in the good sense: it indicates a clean, dependency-ordered build with no visible retries, no compiler-error recovery loops, and no partial rebuilds inflating wall time. The test phase ran 2 suites in 1,099 ms with 0 failures. Two observations matter here. First, the ratio of test time to build time is approximately 1:27 — the suite is fast relative to compilation, which means the expensive part of any future change cycle is the build, not the validation, and iteration strategy should optimize for incremental compilation. Second, the suites completed in just over one second while still being reported as executed rather than skipped, so the green result reflects real assertion work rather than an empty harness short-circuiting. The environment detail worth preserving verbatim is the isolation mode: netns (network namespace). Running under a network namespace removes ambient connectivity as a variable, which strengthens the result for a component like SQLite that must behave identically whether or not a host has external reachability — no test could have silently depended on a network path.",
      "sources": [],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Fix Cycle: Nothing Failed, So Nothing Was Patched",
      "content": "The task specification anticipated a repair loop — \"fix what fails, and confirm the tests\" — but the observed failure count was zero, so the fix stage was legitimately vacuous. It is important not to paper over this distinction. A report that says \"tests passed\" and a report that says \"tests failed, were repaired, and then passed\" describe very different levels of engagement with the codebase, and only the first applies here. No source file was modified, no patch was authored, no regression was isolated, and consequently no risk was introduced by repair activity. The absence of failures also means the third subtask carried a different burden than originally scoped: instead of summarizing root causes and diffs, it had to establish that the green result is trustworthy enough to close on. Three properties support that judgment. The build produced a concrete, countable artifact (63 files, 327,016 lines) rather than a cached or stubbed output. Both declared suites actually ran (2 of 2, not 2 skipped). Execution occurred in a controlled namespace rather than an ad-hoc developer shell. Against those, the honest limitation is coverage breadth: two suites for a combined interpreter-plus-database tree is a narrow surface, and a zero-failure outcome on a narrow surface is weaker evidence than zero failures on an exhaustive one. Any reader treating this brief as a sign-off should weight it accordingly.",
      "sources": [],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
