      "content": "Because further research was prohibited, there is no external baseline to compare this build against — no published reference timings for a stock lua-5.4.7 compile, no upstream SQLite test-suite duration to benchmark the 1,099 ms figure against, and no defect history to contrast with the 0-failure result. The comparison therefore collapses to internal consistency checks, and those all hold. Build cost scales plausibly with input size: 327,016 lines in 30,170 ms sits comfortably in the range one expects for a straightforward C compilation without link-time optimization passes stretching wall time. Test cost is proportionally tiny, which is the correct shape for a suite that exercises interpreter semantics and database operations locally rather than performing long-running stress or fuzz campaigns. Isolation adds overhead in principle — namespace setup, restricted socket availability — yet did not manifest as test flakiness or timeouts, suggesting the suites are self-contained by design and do not reach outside their process boundary. Where the picture is thinner is in dimensionality: the execution report gives counts and durations but not per-suite breakdowns, not assertion totals, and not coverage percentages. Those gaps cannot be closed inside a three-subtask plan, and attempting to infer them would violate the no-further-research constraint. The comparative conclusion is therefore modest and deliberate: this build is internally coherent, correctly isolated, and cheap to re-validate.",
