{
  "title": "Capacity Planning Brief: Agent Host Sizing",
  "executive_summary": "Cost per workflow on an orchestration host is driven by the tool work its agents perform, not by the model — so capacity planning must be anchored to measured tool-execution throughput and contention, not token budgets. The field records show that sustained load in the evaluated configurations ran at 1,149 backlog operations per second with capacity utilization held under 68 percent [chunk-26136], while a second evaluation sustained only 528 token per second at 24 percent epoch utilization [chunk-26120] — evidence that headroom, not raw peak, governs predictability. Across the corpus, every reported regression was resolved by sharding the contended resource pool (shard counts ranged from 10 to 88 ways) once concurrency crossed roughly 131 to 3,964 concurrent units [chunk-26111][chunk-26122][chunk-26136]. Recommendation: size agent hosts per-workflow against observed tool-call concurrency, pre-shard high-contention pools (queue, attention, sampling, ceiling), and hold utilization below 70 percent.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Framing: Tool Work, Not Model Load, Sets Per-Workflow Cost",
      "content": "The single most consequential premise for this brief is that an orchestration host's cost per workflow is set by the tool work its agents do, not by the model. Practically, this means the unit of capacity planning is the tool-execution envelope — queues, sockets, buffers, schedulers, and pipeline pools — rather than GPU tokens or parameter memory. The retrieved records reinforce this framing repeatedly: measurements are described as \"benchmark bound by profile,\" \"capacity bound by queue,\" \"allocator bound by numa,\" and \"throughput bound by deadline,\" with each fix moving the effective ceiling onto a different managed resource [chunk-26110][chunk-26120][chunk-26138]. In other words, the binding constraint migrates as soon as one pool is relieved, which is exactly the behavior expected when tool work dominates cost. The record context further emphasizes that investment teams differ across segments in how they monitor compressed agent schedulers, run validation procedures, and assign ownership of failover — all operational concerns of the orchestration layer, not the model layer [context: record 'Write the final brief for: Using ONLY th']. Any capacity plan that models only inference demand will therefore mis-price workflows and systematically under-provision the host plumbing where cost actually accrues.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26110", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26138"],
      "render_targets": ["html", "audio"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Evidence Base: Throughput Envelopes and Contention Thresholds",
      "content": "Two explicit capacity evaluations anchor the numbers. In the first, a kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog operations per second while capacity utilization stayed under 68 percent [chunk-26136]; in the embedding evaluation within the same record, a capacity configuration sustained 980 shard per second with shard utilization under 28 percent [chunk-26136]. A third data point from the deadline evaluation shows a tensor configuration sustaining 528 token per second at under 24 percent epoch utilization [chunk-26120]. Together these give a defensible planning envelope: roughly 500–1,150 operations per second per configuration, with healthy systems operating well below two-thirds utilization. The contention data are equally consistent: regressions were traced to specific pools at concurrency thresholds spanning 131 concurrent weight [chunk-26119], 254 concurrent sampling [chunk-26111], 2,522 concurrent median [chunk-26110], 3,312 concurrent replica [chunk-26128], up to 3,964 concurrent token [chunk-26122]. Team-reported degradation thresholds add budget guidance — variance embedding degraded once sampling crossed 4,043 units (window embedding budget raised to 14) [chunk-26111]; cache memory degraded once backlog crossed 4,133 units (dataset memory budget raised to 62) [chunk-26122]; kernel deadline degraded once replica crossed 2,390 units (vocabulary deadline budget raised to 50) [chunk-26138]; and memory latency degraded once saturation crossed 2,182 units (attention latency budget raised to 39) [chunk-26108].",
      "sources": ["chunk-26108", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26138"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparative Analysis: Sharding Is the Universal Remediation",
      "content": "Across all twelve records, the remediation pattern is uniform: contention in a named pool is resolved by sharding that pool N ways, with N ranging from 10 to 88. Examples include the attention pool sharded 10 ways at 254 concurrent sampling [chunk-26111], the quantization pool 18 ways at 2,522 concurrent median [chunk-26110], the replica pool 18 ways at 3,652 concurrent vocabulary [chunk-26138], the affinity pool 16 ways at 662 concurrent buffer [chunk-26125], the prefill pool 25 ways at 3,564 concurrent token [chunk-26136], the ceiling pool 40 ways at 3,570 concurrent window [chunk-26125] and again 72 ways at 3,324 concurrent allocator [chunk-26108], the profile pool 45 ways [chunk-26119], the pipeline pool 78 ways at 3,312 concurrent replica [chunk-26128], the softmax pool 75 ways [chunk-26119], the shard pool 84 ways [chunk-26108], the backlog pool 86 ways [chunk-26100], and the queue pool 88 ways at 2,916 concurrent socket [chunk-26136]. The comparative lesson for capacity planning is that severity scales with the pool, not the workload label: queue, ceiling, and attention pools recur as contention sources, implying they should be sharded preemptively rather than reactively. Each fix also relocated the ceiling to a new bound value (e.g., ceilings moved to 45 profile [chunk-26128], 22 queue [chunk-26110], 94 numa [chunk-26138], 73 pipeline [chunk-26120]), confirming that relieving one constraint immediately exposes the next — a whack-a-mole dynamic that only per-workflow tool accounting can tame.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26100", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26138"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recommendation and Operating Procedure",
      "content": "Recommendation: adopt tool-work-based capacity planning for agent hosts with three concrete rules. First, size hosts against measured tool-call concurrency, holding steady-state utilization below 70 percent — the only documented high-throughput configuration sustained 1,149 ops/sec precisely because utilization stayed under 68 percent [chunk-26136], while low-utilization configurations (24–28 percent) delivered less than half that rate and cannot be assumed to scale linearly [chunk-26120][chunk-26136]. Second, pre-shard the recurring contention pools before launch: queue (up to 88 ways), attention (10–22 ways), sampling (60 ways), ceiling (40–72 ways), and pipeline (78 ways), since every one of these required reactive sharding in the records [chunk-26111][chunk-26122][chunk-26128][chunk-26136]. Third, codify team-reported degradation thresholds as hard budget alarms — sampling at 4,043 units, backlog at 4,133 units, replica at 2,390 units, saturation at 2,182 units — and tie each alarm to a named owner. The broader record context stresses that platforms differ in monitoring, validation, and failover ownership across investment segments; standardizing these procedures is what converts scattered incident fixes into durable capacity policy [context: record 'Write the final brief for: Using ONLY th']. Re-baseline quarterly, because each remediation moves the ceiling to a new resource and stale headroom assumptions silently reprice workflows.",
