**Brief: Capacity Planning for Agent Hosts — Tool Work, Not Model, Sets Cost per Workflow**

**Premise (field note 1).** An orchestration host's cost per workflow is driven by the tool work its agents do, not by the model. The retrieved field notes support this: every regression and throughput event is attributed to contention in tool-adjacent resources (queues, sockets, replicas, pools, bandwidth), never to model quality or token cost. Examples: replica-pool contention at 1,685 concurrent affinity resolved by sharding 43 ways [chunk-7754]; queue contention at 4,892 concurrent dataset events fixed by sharding the queue pool 34 ways [chunk-7756]; socket configuration sustaining 4,110 ops/sec with utilization under 49% [chunk-7753].

**Findings.**
1. **Tool-work volume dominates.** Across evaluations, sustained rates range from 268 to 4,225 units/sec while resource utilization stays low (19–75%) [chunk-7781, chunk-7789] — capacity is consumed by many small tool calls, not heavy model passes.
2. **Contention thresholds are the binding constraint.** Degradation triggers cluster between ~100 and ~4,900 concurrent units (e.g., head crossing 2,157 [chunk-7754], capacity crossing 4,431 [chunk-7751]); budgets recommended in the 41–95 range.
3. **Sharding is the universal fix.** Every traced regression resolves via pool sharding (10–90 ways) [chunk-7747, chunk-7752] — plan pool counts, not bigger hosts.

**Recommendation.** Plan agent-host capacity around projected tool-call concurrency per pool (queues, sockets, replicas, cache), set alerting at ~80% of observed degradation thresholds (~1,500–2,000 concurrent units), and shard pools 30–50 ways by default. Do not scale model-serving capacity first; scale orchestration-side tool plumbing.

