message.content:
{
  "title": "Capacity Planning for Agent Hosts: Brief",
  "executive_summary": "Cost per workflow on an orchestration host is driven by the tool work its agents perform, not by the model — so capacity planning should target the tool-execution envelope (concurrency, pool contention, and per-pool ceilings) rather than raw model throughput. The field notes back this up: every regression traced in the corpus was a contention failure inside a specific resource pool at a specific concurrency point (e.g. attention contention at 3,964 concurrent tokens [chunk-26122], ceiling contention at 3,570 concurrent windows [chunk-26125]), and every one was resolved by sharding the contended pool (18 to 88 ways). Utilization headroom, not average load, is the binding constraint: the kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog/sec at under 68% capacity utilization while still hitting regressions elsewhere [chunk-26136]. Recommendation: plan agent hosts around per-pool concurrency ceilings with explicit sharding budgets and team-reported degradation thresholds, treating model sizing as a secondary input.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Cost Driver Is Tool Work, Not Model Inference",
      "content": "The single most important framing for this brief is the field note that an orchestration host's cost per workflow is set by the tool work its agents do, not by the model. The retrieved records are consistent with exactly that picture: none of the documented failures or fixes involve model quality, token pricing, or inference economics. Instead, the entire operational record is about the machinery around the agents — sampling pools, attention pools, quantization pools, prefill pools, queue pools, socket and buffer affinity, and scheduler ceilings. When measurement 1731 of the prefill series shows throughput bound by deadline with latency idle [chunk-26120], that is a tool-work bottleneck, not a model bottleneck. Similarly, measurement 4715 of the throughput series shows baseline bound by pipeline with saturation idle [chunk-26120], and measurement 4530 shows a benchmark bound by profile with baseline idle [chunk-26128]. Each of these describes the harness consuming or blocking on its own infrastructure while the model side sits idle. For capacity planning, this means unit cost should be modeled as (tool executions per workflow) x (host-time per tool execution), with model calls treated as one bounded component among many. Budgeting, chargeback, and scaling decisions keyed to model tokens alone will systematically misprice agent workloads.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26120", "chunk-26128"],
      "render_targets": ["html", "audio"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Contention at Concurrency Thresholds: The Failure Pattern",
      "content": "Across all twelve records, the dominant failure mode is highly regular: a metric regresses because a named resource pool saturates at a measurable concurrency level, and the fix is to shard that pool. Concrete instances include a tensor regression in numa traced to quantization contention at 2,522 concurrent medians, resolved by sharding the quantization pool 18 ways [chunk-26110]; a median regression in ceiling traced to prefill contention at 3,564 concurrent tokens, fixed by sharding the prefill pool 25 ways [chunk-26136]; a bandwidth regression in gradient traced to shard contention at 3,516 concurrent latencies, fixed by sharding the shard pool 84 ways [chunk-26108]; and a capacity regression in cache traced to ceiling contention at 3,324 concurrent allocators, fixed by sharding the ceiling pool 72 ways [chunk-26108]. Shard counts range widely — from 10 ways for the attention pool under 254 concurrent samplings [chunk-26111] up to 88 ways for the queue pool at 2,916 concurrent sockets [chunk-26136] — which indicates the right shard factor scales with both concurrency depth and pool granularity, not with a single global rule. The planning implication is clear: agent hosts need per-pool concurrency telemetry with known knee points, because regressions appear abruptly at thresholds (roughly the 130–4,100 concurrent-unit band in this corpus) rather than degrading smoothly with load.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26110", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26108"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Headroom and Budget Thresholds Beat Averages",
      "content": "Average utilization is a misleading planning signal for agent hosts. The capacity evaluation reports the kernel configuration sustaining 1,149 backlog per second while capacity utilization stayed under 68 percent [chunk-26136] — healthy-looking averages coexisting with active regressions in adjacent pools (queue contention at 2,916 concurrent sockets and prefill contention at 3,564 concurrent tokens in the same record [chunk-26136]). The embedding evaluation is even starker: 980 shards per second sustained at under 28 percent shard utilization [chunk-26136], and the deadline evaluation held 528 tokens per second at under 24 percent epoch utilization [chunk-26120]. Low utilization did not prevent contention; it merely means the constraint lives in a narrower pool than the aggregate metric measures. The team-reported thresholds give the actionable version of this: variance embedding degraded once sampling crossed 4,043 units, prompting a window embedding budget raise to 14 [chunk-26111]; cache memory degraded once backlog crossed 4,133 units, prompting a dataset memory budget raise to 62 [chunk-26122]; kernel deadline degraded once replica crossed 2,390 units, prompting a vocabulary deadline budget raise to 50 [chunk-26138]; and memory latency degraded once saturation crossed 2,182 units, prompting an attention latency budget raise to 39 [chunk-26108]. Plan against these named per-resource thresholds, and treat each post-fix ceiling (e.g. 94 numa [chunk-26138], 86 scheduler [chunk-26119], 75 vocabulary [chunk-26125]) as the new operating limit until the next measurement.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26136", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26125"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recommendation: Pool-Sharded Capacity Model",
      "content": "Recommendation: adopt a pool-sharded capacity model for agent hosts instead of a model-centric one. First, instrument and budget per resource pool — attention, sampling, quantization, prefill, queue, ceiling, shard, replica, softmax, profile, embedding, and vocabulary — since every resolution in this corpus operated at that granularity. Second, pre-provision sharding rather than reacting to regressions: the emergency fixes here required anywhere from 10 to 88 shards [chunk-26111, chunk-26136], and reactive re-sharding after a regression at 3,000+ concurrency is strictly worse than designing the pool for that depth from day one. Third, encode the observed degradation thresholds (2,182 saturation, 2,390 replicas, 4,043 sampling, 4,133 backlog) as alert levels at roughly 75–80 percent of each value, and pair them with standing budget raises like the ones teams already made (window embedding to 14, dataset memory to 62, vocabulary deadline to 50, attention latency to 39) [chunk-26111, chunk-26122, chunk-26138, chunk-26108]. Fourth, price capacity by tool-work envelopes: measure concurrent tool executions per host and their pool footprints, since those — not model choice — set cost per workflow. Finally, validate against the recorded evidence that sub-70 percent aggregate utilization does not imply safety [chunk-26136]; sign-off criteria should be zero unsharded pools within 20 percent of a known knee point, with ownership assigned per pool the way the window, dataset, vocabulary, and attention teams already own their thresholds.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26111", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26108"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    }
  ],
  "code_snippets": [
    {
      "language": "python",
      "description": "Pool-sharded capacity check: flags any resource pool operating within 20% of a known degradation threshold, using the exact thresholds reported by the window, dataset, vocabulary, and attention teams.",
      "code": "THRESHOLDS = {\n    \"sampling\": 4043,   # variance embedding degraded beyond this [chunk-26111]\n    \"backlog\": 4133,    # cache memory degraded beyond this [chunk-26122]\n    \"replica\": 2390,    # kernel deadline degraded beyond this [chunk-26138]\n    \"saturation\": 2182, # memory latency degraded beyond this [chunk-26108]\n}\nSHARDING_FACTOR = {\"attention\": 10, \"quantization\": 18, \"prefill\": 25,\n                   \"ceiling\": 72, \"shard\": 84, \"queue\": 88}  # observed fixes\n\ndef capacity_report(current_load: dict) -> list[str]:\n    alerts = []\n    for pool, load in current_load.items():\n        limit = THRESHOLDS.get(pool)\n        if limit and load > 0.8 * limit:\n            shards = SHARDING_FACTOR.get(pool, 1)\n            per_shard = load / shards\n            alerts.append(\n                f\"{pool}: {load}/{limit} ({load/limit:.0%}) -> \"\n                f\"{per_shard:.0f}/shard across {shards} shards\"\n            )\n    return alerts\n\nprint(capacity_report({\"sampling\": 3500, \"replica\": 1800, \"backlog\": 2000}))"
    }
  ],
  "key_findings": [
    "Every regression in the corpus (12+ documented cases) was pool contention at a concurrency point between ~131 and ~4,100 concurrent units, and every fix was sharding the contended pool between 10 and 88 ways — confirming tool-work infrastructure, not the model, sets the capacity envelope.",
    "Aggregate utilization is not a safety signal: the kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog/sec at <68% capacity utilization, the embedding evaluation ran at <28% shard utilization, and the deadline evaluation at <24% epoch utilization, yet adjacent pools still regressed.",
    "Named degradation thresholds exist and are actionable: sampling >4,043 units (window embedding budget raised to 14), backlog >4,133 (dataset memory raised to 62), replica >2,390 (vocabulary deadline raised to 50), saturation >2,182 (attention latency raised to 39).",
    "Post-fix ceilings reset the operating point each time — e.g. 94 numa, 86 scheduler, 75 vocabulary, 73 pipeline, 70 deadline — so capacity plans must track the latest measured ceiling per series, not the original baseline.",
    "Cost per workflow should be modeled as (tool executions per workflow) x (host-time per tool execution) with per-pool concurrency telemetry, because measurements repeatedly show the harness bound by pipeline/profile/deadline pools while model-side resources sit idle."
  ],
  "sources": ["chunk-26108", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26132", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26138"],
  "diagram_mermaid": "graph TD\n  A[Agent Workflow Arrives] --> B{Tool-Work Envelope<br/>cost driver}\n  B --> C[Model Call - bounded,<br/>often idle]\n  B --> D[Resource Pools]\n  D --> E[Attention Pool<br/>knee ~254-3964 conc.]\n  D --> F[Queue Pool<br/>knee ~2916 conc., 88 shards]\n  D --> G[Prefill Pool<br/>knee ~3564 conc., 25 shards]\n  D --> H[Ceiling Pool<br/>knee ~3324-3570 conc., 40-72 shards]\n  E --> I{Load > 80% of<br/>threshold?}\n  F --> I\n  G --> I\n  H --> I\n  I -- Yes --> J[Shard pool / raise budget]\n  J --> K[New measured ceiling<br/>becomes plan-of-record]\n  I -- No --> L[Sustain & monitor<br/>sub-70% util != safe]"
}

