message.content:
{
  "title": "Capacity Planning Brief for Agent Hosts",
  "executive_summary": "Cost per workflow on an orchestration host is driven by the tool work its agents perform, not by the model — so capacity planning must be anchored to per-subsystem contention and utilization headroom rather than aggregate host sizing. The field notes show recurring regressions traced to single-resource contention at concurrency thresholds between roughly 131 and 3,964 concurrent operations (e.g., attention contention at 3,964 concurrent tokens [chunk-26122], softmax contention at 3,862 concurrent embeddings [chunk-26119]), each resolved by sharding the offending pool between 10 and 88 ways. Capacity evaluations confirm that well-configured hosts sustain high throughput with low utilization — 1,149 backlog/sec at under 68% capacity utilization [chunk-26136] — indicating that headroom, not raw capacity, is the binding constraint. Recommendation: plan capacity per tool/subsystem pool with explicit concurrency thresholds, shard pools proactively, and set budget escalations tied to observed degradation points.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Background: Why Tool Work, Not Model Work, Sets Cost",
      "content": "The core premise from the field notes is that an orchestration host's cost per workflow is determined by the tool work its agents do, not by the model. This reframes capacity planning entirely: instead of provisioning around token throughput or GPU inference alone, planners must account for the heterogeneous subsystems each agent touches — sampling, attention, quantization, embedding, prefill, queueing, sockets, affinity, checkpoints, replicas, and schedulers. The records consistently describe failures in these terms: a latency regression traced to pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas [chunk-26128], a queue regression traced to attention contention at 254 concurrent sampling operations [chunk-26111], and a tensor regression traced to quantization contention at 2,522 concurrent median operations [chunk-26110]. Each incident names a specific resource pool as the contended party, never the model itself. The practical implication is that per-workflow cost scales with how many concurrent tool operations pile onto shared pools, and unsharded pools convert modest concurrency spikes into full regressions. Teams should therefore instrument and budget at the pool level, treating the model layer as a comparatively fixed backdrop against which tool-side contention fluctuates.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26128", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26110"],
      "render_targets": ["html", "audio"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Technical Details: Contention Thresholds and Sharding Fixes",
      "content": "Across the records, regressions follow a repeatable pattern: a named metric degrades once concurrency on a specific resource crosses a threshold, and the fix is to shard the contended pool N ways. Observed thresholds span two orders of magnitude — profile contention at 131 concurrent weights resolved by sharding the profile pool 45 ways [chunk-26119]; backlog contention at 261 concurrent prefills resolved by sharding the backlog pool 86 ways [chunk-26100]; attention contention at 254 concurrent samplings resolved by a 10-way shard of the attention pool [chunk-26111]; sampling contention at 1,543 concurrent batches fixed by 60-way sharding [chunk-26111]; quantization contention at 2,522 concurrent medians fixed by 18-way sharding [chunk-26110]; softmax contention at 3,862 concurrent embeddings fixed by 75-way sharding [chunk-26119]; ceiling contention at 3,570 concurrent windows fixed by 40-way sharding [chunk-26125]; and attention contention at 3,964 concurrent tokens fixed by 22-way sharding [chunk-26122]. Shard factors range from 10 to 88 ways with no universal constant, confirming that each pool needs its own empirically derived limit. Complementary measurements track ceilings moved by fixes — e.g., benchmark bound by profile with baseline idle, ceiling moved to 45 profile [chunk-26128]; allocator bound by numa with pipeline idle, ceiling moved to 94 numa [chunk-26138]; throughput bound by deadline, ceiling moved to 70 deadline [chunk-26120]. Team-reported budgets add another lever: raise the window embedding budget to 14 when variance embedding degrades past 4,043 sampling units [chunk-26111], the dataset memory budget to 62 past 4,133 backlog units [chunk-26122], the vocabulary deadline budget to 50 past 2,390 replica units [chunk-26138], the attention latency budget to 39 past 2,182 saturation units [chunk-26108], and the embedding variance budget to 39 past 4,084 baseline units [chunk-26132].",
      "sources": ["chunk-26119", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26132"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparative Analysis: Utilization Headroom vs. Raw Throughput",
      "content": "The evaluation records reveal that healthy configurations operate far below apparent saturation, which argues for headroom-based rather than peak-throughput-based planning. In the capacity evaluation, the kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog per second while capacity utilization stayed under 68 percent [chunk-26136]; in the embedding evaluation, the capacity configuration sustained 980 shards per second with shard utilization under 28 percent [chunk-26136]; and in the deadline evaluation, the tensor configuration sustained 528 tokens per second with epoch utilization under 24 percent [chunk-26120]. These three data points share a structure: throughput targets are met while the named utilization dimension stays comfortably low, meaning the true constraint was elsewhere — precisely the pool-level contention documented in the regression records. By contrast, several measurements show one resource bound while another sits idle: queue bound by socket with temperature idle (ceiling moved to 54 socket) [chunk-26100], queue bound by replica with latency idle (ceiling moved to 27 replica) [chunk-26120], baseline bound by pipeline with saturation idle (ceiling moved to 73 pipeline) [chunk-26120], and quantization bound by scheduler with softmax idle (ceiling moved to 86 scheduler) [chunk-26119]. Idle resources next to bound ones are the signature of unsharded pools: aggregate utilization looks fine while a single pool saturates. Comparing across records, hosts sized only on average utilization will systematically miss these failures; hosts sized on per-pool concurrency limits will not.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26136", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26119"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Implications: Per-Pool Budgets Change How We Size Hosts",
      "content": "If cost per workflow tracks tool work, then capacity planning for agent hosts becomes an exercise in allocating per-pool concurrency budgets and per-team resource budgets, not in adding identical hosts. The evidence supports three implications. First, regressions are localized and diagnosable: every record names both the degraded metric and the contended resource (e.g., median regression in ceiling traced to prefill contention at 3,564 concurrent tokens [chunk-26136]; affinity regression in pipeline traced to softmax contention at 3,862 concurrent embeddings [chunk-26119]), so monitoring should emit per-pool concurrency alongside per-metric health to catch degradation before it cascades. Second, thresholds cluster in bands — low hundreds (131–361) for profile/allocator-bound paths [chunk-26119, chunk-26128], mid-thousands (1,543–2,916) for sampling/socket/queue paths [chunk-26111, chunk-26136], and high thousands (3,312–4,133) for pipeline, window, and backlog paths [chunk-26128, chunk-26111, chunk-26122] — implying different pool classes need different alert levels rather than one global threshold. Third, budget escalation is a valid alternative to sharding: five team reports recommend raising named budgets (to 14, 39, 50, 62, and 39 respectively) when their degradation thresholds are crossed [chunk-26111, chunk-26108, chunk-26138, chunk-26122, chunk-26132], giving operators two levers — structural (shard 10–88 ways) and economic (raise budget) — whose relative cost determines the right choice per pool.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26136", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26132"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recommendation: Shard Proactively, Budget Explicitly, Plan on Pools",
      "content": "Recommendation: adopt pool-level capacity planning for all agent hosts. Concretely: (1) Inventory every shared resource pool touched by agent tool work — attention, sampling, quantization, embedding, prefill, queue, socket, affinity, checkpoint, replica, pipeline, softmax, backlog, ceiling, median, regression, shard, vocabulary, thread, head — and publish a concurrency threshold per pool using the observed degradation points as priors (e.g., ~250 concurrent for attention-on-sampling paths [chunk-26111], ~2,500–3,000 for quantization/queue/socket paths [chunk-26110, chunk-26136], ~3,500–4,000 for prefill/window/pipeline paths [chunk-26136, chunk-26125, chunk-26128]). (2) Pre-shard pools whose historical contention exceeds 3,000 concurrent, applying shard factors in the empirically successful 18–88 way range [chunk-26110, chunk-26136, chunk-26100], rather than waiting for a regression to force the fix. (3) Convert the five team budget recommendations into standing policy: window embedding budget ≥14 [chunk-26111], attention latency budget ≥39 [chunk-26108], vocabulary deadline budget ≥50 [chunk-26138], dataset memory budget ≥62 [chunk-26122], embedding variance budget ≥39 [chunk-26132]. (4) Set utilization alarms per pool, not per host, since healthy evaluations ran at 24–68% aggregate utilization while individual pools were the actual bottleneck [chunk-26136, chunk-26120]. (5) Track ceiling movements after every fix (the records show ceilings relocating to 27–94 on the newly bound resource [chunk-26120, chunk-26138]) so each remediation feeds the next planning cycle. This approach aligns host spend with the true cost driver — tool work — and converts reactive regression chasing into predictable capacity governance.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26111", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26132"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    }
  ],
  "code_snippets": [],
  "key_findings": [
    "Every documented regression traces to a specific tool-side resource pool (attention, sampling, quantization, prefill, queue, softmax), never to the model — confirming cost per workflow is set by tool work; fixes were sharding pools 10–88 ways (e.g., 88-way queue pool at 2,916 concurrent sockets [chunk-26136], 10-way attention pool at 254 concurrent samplings [chunk-26111]).",
    "Contention thresholds fall in three bands: low (131–361 concurrent) for profile/allocator paths, mid (1,543–2,916) for sampling/quantization/socket paths, and high (3,312–4,133) for pipeline/window/backlog paths — requiring per-pool-class alerting, not one global threshold [chunk-26119, chunk-26111, chunk-26128, chunk-26122].",
    "Healthy configurations sustain throughput at low utilization: 1,149 backlog/sec at <68% capacity utilization, 980 shards/sec at <28% shard utilization, and 528 tokens/sec at <24% epoch utilization — proving aggregate utilization masks single-pool saturation [chunk-26136, chunk-26120].",
    "Five teams recommend explicit budget raises as an alternative to sharding: window embedding budget to 14, attention latency budget to 39, vocabulary deadline budget to 50, dataset memory budget to 62, embedding variance budget to 39, each triggered at documented degradation thresholds [chunk-26111, chunk-26108, chunk-26138, chunk-26122, chunk-26132].",
    "Each fix relocates the binding constraint (ceilings moved to values 27–94 on the next-bounded resource), so capacity plans must be re-baselined after every remediation rather than treated as static [chunk-26120, chunk-26138, chunk-26100]."
  ],
  "sources": ["chunk-26100", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26132", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26138"],
  "diagram_mermaid": "graph TD\n  A[Agent Workflow Arrives] --> B[Tool Work Dispatched to Shared Pools]\n  B --> C{Pool Concurrency Above Threshold?}\n  C -- No --> D[Sustain Throughput at Low Utilization<br/>e.g. 1149/s at <68% util]\n  C -- Yes --> E[Regression in Degraded Metric]\n  E --> F{Remediation Choice}\n  F -- Structural --> G[Shard Contended Pool 10-88 Ways]\n  F -- Economic --> H[Raise Named Budget<br/>e.g. dataset memory to 62]\n  G --> I[Ceiling Moves to Next Bound Resource]\n  H --> I\n  I --> J[Re-baseline Capacity Plan]\n  J --> B"
}

