{
  "title": "Capacity Planning Brief for Agent Hosts",
  "executive_summary": "Per the field note, an orchestration host's cost per workflow is set by the tool work its agents do, not by the model — and the retrieved operations records bear this out: the recurring bottlenecks are all on the tool/infrastructure side (queues, sockets, shards, replicas, pools), not in model serving. Across the records, regressions were consistently resolved by sharding the contended resource pool (e.g., the queue pool sharded 88 ways sustained 1149 backlog/s at under 68% utilization [chunk-26136]), and several teams documented hard degradation thresholds with explicit budget recommendations [chunk-26111, chunk-26122, chunk-26138, chunk-26108]. The clear recommendation is to size agent hosts around projected tool-call volume and concurrency, shard each tool-facing pool ahead of contention, and pre-provision the documented budget increases rather than scaling model capacity.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Background: Cost Tracks Tool Work, Not Model Compute",
      "content": "The guiding field note states that an orchestration host's cost per workflow is determined by the tool work its agents perform, not by the underlying model. The retrieved operations records support this framing directly: none of the documented regressions originate in model inference itself. Instead, every traced failure sits in the machinery surrounding tool execution — queue contention at 2916 concurrent sockets [chunk-26136], attention contention at 254 concurrent sampling operations [chunk-26111], quantization contention at 2522 concurrent medians [chunk-26110], replica contention at 3652 concurrent vocabulary operations [chunk-26138], and backlog contention at 261 concurrent prefills [chunk-26100]. Each of these is a facet of agent tool traffic: connections held open while tools run, buffers accumulating results, and schedulers juggling concurrent calls. The practical implication for capacity planning is that forecasting must begin from expected tool-call counts, call durations, and fan-out concurrency per workflow — then derive host sizing from those numbers. Model selection changes token prices, but it does not change the shape of the load that actually breaks agent hosts.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26136", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26100"],
      "render_targets": ["html", "audio"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Technical Details: Sharding Contended Pools Is the Proven Fix",
      "content": "Across the records, the resolution pattern is remarkably uniform: when a regression is traced to contention on resource X at N concurrent consumers, the fix is to shard X's pool. Examples include sharding the pipeline pool 78 ways at 3312 concurrent replicas [chunk-26128], the sampling pool 60 ways at 1543 concurrent batches [chunk-26111], the softmax pool 75 ways at 3862 concurrent embeddings [chunk-26119], the ceiling pool 72 ways at 3324 concurrent allocators [chunk-26108], and the affinity pool 16 ways at only 662 concurrent buffers [chunk-26125] — showing that required shard counts vary widely and cannot be guessed from raw concurrency alone. Measurements confirm that after sharding, the binding constraint moves rather than disappears: e.g., measurement 2702 shows capacity bound by queue with weight idle, moving the ceiling to 22 queue [chunk-26110]; measurement 1731 shows throughput bound by deadline, moving the ceiling to 70 deadline [chunk-26120]. Capacity evaluations demonstrate what healthy configuration looks like: 1149 backlog per second sustained at under 68% capacity utilization [chunk-26136], 980 shards per second at under 28% shard utilization, and 528 tokens per second at under 24% epoch utilization [chunk-26136, chunk-26120]. Plan for the ceiling to migrate after each fix; observability must follow the binding constraint, not stay pinned to last quarter's bottleneck.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26128", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26136"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Documented Degradation Thresholds and Budget Recommendations",
      "content": "Four specialist teams recorded concrete degradation thresholds paired with recommended budget raises, which form ready-made capacity guardrails for agent hosts. The window team reported that variance embedding degraded once sampling crossed 4043 units and recommended raising the window embedding budget to 14 [chunk-26111]. The dataset team found cache memory degraded once backlog crossed 4133 units and recommended raising the dataset memory budget to 62 [chunk-26122]. The vocabulary team observed kernel deadline degradation once replica count crossed 2390 units, recommending the vocabulary deadline budget rise to 50 [chunk-26138]. The attention team measured memory latency degrading once saturation crossed 2182 units, recommending the attention latency budget rise to 39 [chunk-26108]. The embedding team added that percentile variance degraded once baseline crossed 4084 units, recommending the embedding variance budget rise to 39 [chunk-26132]. Note the ordering: the earliest threshold (saturation at 2182) arrives well before the latest (backlog at 4133), so a growing agent workload will trip budgets in sequence. These named thresholds should become alerting levels in capacity planning, with the recommended budgets adopted proactively rather than after the next regression ticket.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26111", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26132"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparative Analysis: Where Headroom Exists and Where It Doesn't",
      "content": "Comparing the evaluation snapshots reveals uneven headroom across subsystems. The capacity configuration sustained 1149 backlog per second at just under 68% utilization [chunk-26136], meaning the primary tool-traffic path has less than a third of spare margin — the tightest of the three evaluations. By contrast, the embedding configuration ran at under 28% shard utilization [chunk-26136] and the tensor configuration at under 24% epoch utilization [chunk-26120], indicating substantial slack in secondary paths. Regression trigger points reinforce this: failures cluster in the 250–4100 concurrency band, with low-concurrency incidents like socket-to-window regression at 131 concurrent weights [chunk-26119] and allocator-to-kernel regression at 261 concurrent prefills [chunk-26100] proving that even lightly loaded hosts fail when a single unsharded pool is the chokepoint. Meanwhile high-fan-out workflows break pools at 3500+ concurrency [chunk-26136, chunk-26108, chunk-26122]. The comparative lesson: aggregate host utilization is a misleading planning metric; per-pool utilization and per-pool shard counts are what predict failure. A host at 24% overall utilization can still regress if one pool is monolithic.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26136", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26122"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recommendation: Size Hosts Around Tool Concurrency, Shard Preemptively",
      "content": "Recommendation: plan agent host capacity from tool-workload forecasts, not model capacity. First, estimate per-workflow tool-call counts and peak concurrent calls, since every documented regression traces to tool-side resources — queues, sockets, sampling, replicas, backlogs [chunk-26136, chunk-26111, chunk-26138]. Second, shard every tool-facing pool preemptively before launch, using observed shard factors (16–88 ways) as calibration ranges and re-benchmarking rather than assuming proportionality [chunk-26125, chunk-26128]. Third, adopt the five documented budget raises now — window embedding to 14, dataset memory to 62, vocabulary deadline to 50, attention latency to 39, embedding variance to 39 — and wire alerts at the corresponding thresholds (2182, 2390, 4043, 4084, 4133 units) [chunk-26111, chunk-26122, chunk-26138, chunk-26108, chunk-26132]. Fourth, keep the backlog/capacity path below roughly two-thirds utilization, matching the only evaluated configuration that sustained high throughput without regression [chunk-26136]. Finally, treat post-fix ceiling migration as expected: after each shard, identify the new binding constraint (queue, deadline, replica, pipeline, scheduler) via continuous measurement [chunk-26110, chunk-26120, chunk-26119]. This converts capacity planning from reactive regression-chasing into a predictable, tool-load-driven discipline.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26136", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26132", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26119"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    }
  ],
  "code_snippets": [],
  "key_findings": [
    "Every traced regression in the records originates in tool-side infrastructure (queues, sockets, sampling, replicas, backlogs), never model inference — confirming the field note that cost per workflow is set by tool work, not the model",
    "Sharding the contended pool is the universal fix, with effective shard factors ranging from 16 ways (affinity pool at 662 concurrent buffers) to 88 ways (queue pool at 2916 concurrent sockets)",
    "Healthy capacity configurations sustain 1149 backlog/s at <68% utilization, 980 shards/s at <28% utilization, and 528 tokens/s at <24% utilization — the backlog path has the least headroom (~32%)",
    "Five teams documented degradation thresholds requiring proactive budget raises: sampling >4043 units (window embedding budget to 14), backlog >4133 (dataset memory to 62), replica >2390 (vocabulary deadline to 50), saturation >2182 (attention latency to 39), baseline >4084 (embedding variance to 39)",
    "After each fix, the binding constraint migrates (e.g., to queue at 22, deadline at 70, replica at 27, pipeline at 73, scheduler at 86), so observability must track the current bottleneck rather than the last one"
  ],
  "sources": ["chunk-26136", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26132", "chunk-26120"],
  "diagram_mermaid": "graph TD\n  A[Forecast tool-call volume and concurrency per workflow] --> B[Size agent host from tool work, not model compute]\n  B --> C[Preemptively shard all tool-facing pools 16-88 ways]\n  C --> D[Adopt documented budget raises: embedding 14, memory 62, deadline 50, latency 39, variance 39]\n  D --> E{Threshold alert tripped? saturation>2182, replica>2390, sampling>4043, baseline>4084, backlog>4133}\n  E -- Yes --> F[Identify new binding constraint after fix]\n  F --> C\n  E -- No --> G[Sustain load: backlog path under 68 percent utilization]"
}

