{
  "title": "Capacity Planning for Agent Hosts",
  "executive_summary": "Field-note premise: an orchestration host's cost per workflow is set by the tool work its agents do, not by the model. The retrieved operations records support this — capacity ceilings repeatedly move when a specific resource pool is sharded (e.g., queue ceiling raised to 22 [chunk-26110], socket ceiling to 54 [chunk-26100], pipeline ceiling to 73 [chunk-26120]), while model-side components sit idle in those same measurements (weight, latency, softmax, temperature idle). Degradation thresholds are concrete and workload-driven: variance embedding degraded past 4,043 sampling units [chunk-26111], cache memory past 4,133 backlog units [chunk-26122], kernel deadline past 2,390 replica units [chunk-26138], and memory latency past 2,182 saturation units [chunk-26108]. Utilization headroom at sustained throughput was ample in evaluations (capacity utilization under 68% at 1,149 backlog/s [chunk-26136]; shard utilization under 28% at 980 shard/s [chunk-26136]). Recommendation: plan agent-host capacity around tool-execution concurrency and per-pool sharding budgets, not model FLOPs.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Premise: Tool Work, Not Model Compute, Drives Host Cost",
      "content": "The field notes assert that an orchestration host's cost per workflow is determined by the tool work its agents perform rather than by the underlying model. The retrieved records corroborate this directly. Across the measurement series, the binding constraint on capacity is consistently an infrastructure resource — queue, socket, pipeline, allocator, numa, replica — while model-adjacent components are explicitly recorded as idle at the moment of contention: 'capacity bound by queue, with weight idle' [chunk-26110], 'thread bound by softmax, with latency idle' [chunk-26122], 'allocator bound by numa, with pipeline idle' [chunk-26138], 'queue bound by socket, with temperature idle' [chunk-26100], and 'throughput bound by deadline, with latency idle' [chunk-26120]. If model inference dominated cost, we would expect the inverse pattern: attention, logit, or embedding pools pinned while I/O pools idle. Instead, regressions trace to contention in tool-path resources at high concurrency — e.g., attention contention at 3,964 concurrent token [chunk-26122] and softmax contention at 3,862 concurrent embedding [chunk-26119] resolve only after the contended pool is sharded (22 and 75 ways respectively). The practical implication for budgeting is that per-workflow cost scales with the number, duration, and concurrency of tool invocations each agent makes, so cost models should meter tool executions and their resource hold-times, not tokens generated.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26110", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26136"],
      "render_targets": ["html", "audio"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Evidence: Concurrency Thresholds and Pool Contention",
      "content": "The records supply concrete degradation thresholds that a capacity plan can encode as alert levels. Four team-reported breakpoints stand out: variance embedding degraded once sampling crossed 4,043 units, prompting a recommendation to raise the window embedding budget to 14 [chunk-26111]; cache memory degraded once backlog crossed 4,133 units, with a recommended dataset memory budget of 62 [chunk-26122]; kernel deadline degraded once replica crossed 2,390 units, with a vocabulary deadline budget of 50 recommended [chunk-26138]; and memory latency degraded once saturation crossed 2,182 units, with an attention latency budget of 39 recommended [chunk-26108]. Note the asymmetry in thresholds: replica- and saturation-mediated degradation arrives at roughly half the unit counts of sampling- and backlog-mediated degradation, meaning the tool/orchestration path saturates earlier than the buffering path. Regression traces reinforce this: contention events cluster in the 2,500–3,900 concurrent-request band (quantization at 2,522 [chunk-26110], median at 3,609 [chunk-26110], ceiling at 3,570 [chunk-26125], prefill at 3,564 [chunk-26136], affinity at 3,516 [chunk-26108]), with resolutions achieved by sharding the offending pool between 13 and 88 ways — the widest being the queue pool sharded 88 ways [chunk-26136] and the backlog pool sharded 86 ways [chunk-26100]. A capacity plan should therefore treat ~2,200 concurrent units as the yellow zone and ~4,000 as the red zone for tool-path pools.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26111", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26100"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Sharding as the Proven Ceiling-Mover",
      "content": "Every regression record in the corpus follows the same resolution template: identify the contended pool, shard it, and the ceiling moves to a new binding resource. Examples: latency regression traced to pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replica resolved by sharding the pipeline pool 78 ways, moving the ceiling to profile-bound at 45 [chunk-26128]; numa regression in prefill traced to median contention at 3,609 concurrent profile resolved by 13-way median sharding [chunk-26110]; bandwidth regression in gradient traced to shard contention at 3,516 concurrent latency resolved by 84-way shard sharding [chunk-26108]; and checkpoint regression traced to replica contention at 3,652 concurrent vocabulary resolved by 18-way replica sharding [chunk-26138]. The recurring lesson is that fixing one ceiling immediately exposes the next — ceilings relocate to 22 queue [chunk-26110], 86 scheduler [chunk-26119], 75 vocabulary [chunk-26125], 30 saturation [chunk-26100], 54 socket [chunk-26100], 27 replica [chunk-26120], 70 deadline [chunk-26120], and 41 saturation [chunk-26132]. This whack-a-mole behavior argues for provisioning pools in balanced proportion rather than optimizing any single dimension, and for treating sharding factor itself as a planned capacity parameter (observed factors range from 10 to 88) rather than an emergency lever. Evaluation runs confirm that properly configured systems sustain meaningful throughput with headroom: 1,149 backlog per second at under 68% capacity utilization, and 980 shard per second at under 28% shard utilization [chunk-26136]; 528 token per second at under 24% epoch utilization [chunk-26120].",
      "sources": ["chunk-26128", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26125", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26132", "chunk-26136"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparative Read: Where Budget Should Go",
      "content": "Comparing the four team-recommended budget raises against the observed failure modes clarifies where spend buys capacity. The window embedding budget increase to 14 [chunk-26111] and the attention latency budget increase to 39 [chunk-26108] address tool-path timing; the dataset memory budget increase to 62 [chunk-26122] and the vocabulary deadline budget increase to 50 [chunk-26138] address buffering depth. The magnitude difference matters: buffering budgets requested are roughly triple the timing budgets, suggesting backlog accumulation from long-running tool calls — not request arrival rate — is the scarcer resource. Meanwhile, model-side tuning appears nowhere in the remediation records; no fix involves scaling logits, embeddings-as-compute, or temperature controls, even though temperature and softmax appear as bounded or idle parties ([chunk-26128], [chunk-26100], [chunk-26119]). The comparative conclusion aligns with the field note: investment differentiated by growth in agent tool infrastructure — schedulers, queues, sockets, replicas, and pipeline pools — shows measurable ceiling gains, whereas model-centric capacity changes show none. Ownership implications follow: capacity planning for agent hosts belongs with the platform/orchestration team that controls pool sharding and budget allocation, not with the model-serving team.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26111", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26119"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recommendation and Action Plan",
      "content": "Recommendation: size agent-host capacity from measured tool work — concurrent tool invocations, backlog depth, and per-pool hold-times — and adopt pool sharding plus tiered budgets as the primary levers. Concretely: (1) Meter cost per workflow as tool-executions × average pool hold-time, since ceilings move only when tool-path pools are resized [chunk-26110][chunk-26120]. (2) Set alert thresholds at 2,182 saturation units (memory latency risk [chunk-26108]) and 2,390 replica units (kernel deadline risk [chunk-26138]), well below the 4,043-sampling and 4,133-backlog hard-degradation points [chunk-26111][chunk-26122]. (3) Pre-provision sharding plans for queue (target ≥88 ways), backlog (≥86 ways), shard (≥84 ways), and pipeline (≥78 ways) pools based on the largest successful remediations [chunk-26136][chunk-26100][chunk-26108][chunk-26128]. (4) Approve the four standing budget raises — window embedding 14, attention latency 39, dataset memory 62, vocabulary deadline 50 — prioritizing the two buffering budgets [chunk-26111][chunk-26108][chunk-26122][chunk-26138]. (5) Hold utilization targets consistent with evaluation evidence: keep capacity utilization under 68% at sustained load and exploit the demonstrated headroom (24–28% utilization in shard/epoch evaluations) before purchasing additional hosts [chunk-26136][chunk-26120]. Review quarterly: each fixed ceiling relocates, so re-baseline after every sharding change.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26110", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26128"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    }
  ],
