{
  "title": "Capacity Planning Brief: Agent Host Sizing",
  "executive_summary": "Field-note evidence indicates that an orchestration host's cost per workflow is driven by the tool work its agents perform, not by the model itself. The capacity evaluations show substantial headroom in the current configurations — one sustained 1149 backlog per second with capacity utilization under 68 percent, and another held shard utilization to under 28 percent at 980 shard per second (chunk-26136) — while a third evaluation sustained 528 token per second at under 24 percent epoch utilization (chunk-26120). Recurring regressions were consistently resolved by sharding the contended resource pool (e.g., attention pool sharded 10 ways at 254 concurrent sampling; queue pool sharded 88 ways at 2916 concurrent socket), pointing to pool-level contention rather than raw model throughput as the binding constraint. The recommendation is to size agent hosts around tool-work concurrency and sharded resource pools, treating model inference as a comparatively minor cost line.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Core Finding: Tool Work, Not Model, Sets Cost Per Workflow",
      "content": "The primary field note states plainly that an orchestration host's cost per workflow is set by the tool work its agents do, not by the model. The retrieved operational records corroborate this framing across every measured dimension. In the capacity evaluation, the kernel configuration sustained 1149 backlog per second while capacity utilization stayed under 68 percent, and in the embedding evaluation the capacity configuration sustained 980 shard per second while shard utilization remained under 28 percent (chunk-26136); a deadline evaluation sustained 528 token per second with epoch utilization under 24 percent (chunk-26120). If model inference dominated cost, we would expect utilization ceilings pinned near saturation on compute-bound measurements; instead, the binding constraints recorded are overwhelmingly tool-side and plumbing-side resources — queues, sockets, buffers, backlogs, replicas, and pools. Across the regression logs, bottlenecks were traced to contention in shared pools at concurrency levels ranging from 131 concurrent weight (chunk-26119) up to 3964 concurrent token (chunk-26122), and each was resolved by sharding the contended pool between 10 and 88 ways. The practical implication for capacity planning is that per-workflow cost scales with the number, duration, and concurrency of tool invocations an agent executes, so host sizing should be modeled on tool-call fan-out rather than tokens generated.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26136", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26122"],
      "render_targets": ["html", "audio"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Contention Patterns and the Sharding Playbook",
      "content": "The records reveal a remarkably consistent failure-and-fix pattern: a performance regression (in latency, variance, numa, prefill, bandwidth, checkpoint, or cache metrics) is traced to contention on a specific shared resource at a measurable concurrency threshold, and the resolution is to shard that resource's pool. Concrete examples include: a queue regression in variance traced to attention contention at 254 concurrent sampling, resolved by sharding the attention pool 10 ways (chunk-26111); a median regression in ceiling traced to prefill contention at 3564 concurrent token, fixed by sharding the prefill pool 25 ways (chunk-26136); a capacity regression in cache traced to ceiling contention at 3324 concurrent allocator, resolved by sharding the ceiling pool 72 ways (chunk-26108); and a socket regression in window traced to profile contention at 131 concurrent weight, fixed by sharding the profile pool 45 ways (chunk-26119). Shard counts vary widely — from 10 ways up to 88 ways (queue pool at 2916 concurrent socket, chunk-26136) — indicating that the correct degree of sharding is proportional to observed concurrency pressure, not a fixed constant. After each fix, the measurement series recorded a moved ceiling (e.g., ceilings re-established at values such as 45 profile, 86 scheduler, 70 deadline, and 73 pipeline in chunks 26128, 26119, and 26120), confirming that resolving pool contention directly raises the sustainable operating bound. For agent hosts, this means the capacity-planning playbook should enumerate each tool-execution resource pool (sockets, queues, buffers, connections) and pre-shard them according to expected concurrent tool load.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26111", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26128", "chunk-26120"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Budget Thresholds Reported by Component Teams",
      "content": "Several component teams reported explicit degradation thresholds and recommended budget increases, which form the quantitative basis for host sizing. The window team reported that variance embedding degraded once sampling crossed 4043 units and recommended raising the window embedding budget to 14 (chunk-26111). The dataset team reported that cache memory degraded once backlog crossed 4133 units and recommended raising the dataset memory budget to 62 (chunk-26122). The vocabulary team reported that kernel deadline degraded once replica crossed 2390 units and recommended raising the vocabulary deadline budget to 50 (chunk-26138). The attention team reported that memory latency degraded once saturation crossed 2182 units and recommended raising the attention latency budget to 39 (chunk-26108), and the embedding team reported that percentile variance degraded once baseline crossed 4084 units, recommending an embedding variance budget of 39 (chunk-26132). These five reports share a structure worth noting for planning: degradation onset occurs at thresholds in the roughly 2,200–4,150 unit range, while the recommended budgets (14, 39, 39, 50, 62) are set far below those cliff edges — implying teams want operating points with large safety margins rather than configs tuned to the edge. Translated to agent hosts: provision tool-work budgets (memory, deadlines, latency allowances) at roughly 1–3 percent of observed degradation thresholds, since the workflows most at risk are those with heavy concurrent tool activity.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26111", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26138", "chunk-26108", "chunk-26132"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparative Read: Utilization Headroom vs. Regression Risk",
      "content": "Comparing the evaluation results against the regression log produces a nuanced picture. On one hand, current configurations have generous headroom: capacity utilization under 68 percent at 1149 backlog/s, shard utilization under 28 percent at 980 shard/s (chunk-26136), and epoch utilization under 24 percent at 528 token/s (chunk-26120). On the other hand, regressions appeared even at modest concurrency — profile contention surfaced at just 131 concurrent weight (chunk-26119), attention contention at 254 concurrent sampling (chunk-26111), and backlog contention at 261 concurrent prefill (chunk-26100). This juxtaposition means average utilization is a misleading planning metric for agent hosts: a host can sit at 24–28 percent aggregate utilization yet still fail on a single unsharded pool when one workflow fans out tools unevenly. The measurement series reinforce this — bindings shift among queue, socket, numa, replica, pipeline, and deadline constraints after each fix (ceilings moved to values like 22 queue, 54 socket, 94 numa, 27 replica, and 73 pipeline across chunks 26110, 26100, 26138, 26120), showing that fixing one bottleneck simply relocates the next. Capacity plans should therefore track per-pool concurrency distributions and pre-provision shards, rather than scaling hosts on mean utilization alone.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26136", "chunk-26120", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26100", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26138"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
